const SITE_PAGES = [{"slug": "home", "title": "Cranfield University Ultimate", "hero": null, "html": "\n<p class=\"lede\">Welcome to Cranfield Ultimate, the university's ultimate frisbee club. New players are always welcome: come to an open session and we'll teach you everything.</p>\n<h2>When and where</h2>\n<div class=\"cards\">\n  <div class=\"card\"><h3>Outdoor training: open to all</h3><p><strong>Thursdays, 6–8pm</strong><br>Sports pitches by the tennis courts, opposite Chilver Hall.</p></div>\n  <div class=\"card\"><h3>Indoor training: invite only</h3><p><strong>Tuesdays, 7–8:30pm</strong><br>Sports Hall. For players who have been playing a while.</p></div>\n</div>\n<p><a class=\"btn-link\" href=\"https://maps.google.com/?q=Sports+Hall,+Cranfield+University,+College+Rd,+Cranfield,+Bedford+MK43+0AL\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sports Hall on Google Maps ↗</a></p>\n<h2>Find your way around</h2>\n<div class=\"cards\">\n  <a class=\"card\" href=\"#glossary\"><h3>New to ultimate?</h3><p>Start with the glossary, then learn the backhand and forehand.</p></a>\n  <a class=\"card\" href=\"#offence\"><h3>Offence</h3><p>Vert stack, horo, ISO and end zone plays, with diagrams.</p></a>\n  <a class=\"card\" href=\"#defence\"><h3>Defence</h3><p>Switching, zones, clamps, floods and boxing out.</p></a>\n  <a class=\"card\" href=\"#training\"><h3>Training</h3><p>Session structure, warm-ups, this season's plan and 40+ drills.</p></a>\n  <a class=\"card\" href=\"#fitness\"><h3>Fitness</h3><p>Daily Bassline, Tiger/Grizzly/Raptor and the Road to Lecco programme.</p></a>\n  <a class=\"card\" href=\"#rosters\"><h3>Rosters &amp; kit</h3><p>Past teams, awards and kit designs.</p></a>\n</div>\n<h2>Bored between practices?</h2>\n<p>Work through the Centurion throwing routine from AU+ (Advanced Ultimate Coaching).</p>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"AOiyhY8oJno\" data-label=\"AU+ Centurion throwing routine\"></div>"}, {"slug": "glossary", "title": "Glossary", "hero": "6d724e4db0034942119980a92a13e469", "html": "<p class=\"lede\">The words you'll hear at training and tournaments.</p><dl class=\"glossary\"><dt>Break Mark Throw</dt><dd>A throw that goes to the area of the field that the marker is trying to prevent throws from going. It is a throw that “breaks the mark”</dd><dt>Callahan Goal</dt><dd>When a defensive player catches a pass made by the offense, and intended for another offensive player, inside the goal that the defensive team is attempting to score in.</dd><dt>Clear</dt><dd>A cut with the intention of opening space for a teammate, not with the intention of getting the disc. This generally applies following a cut when s/he has not been thrown the disc, or after s/he has thrown a short pass to a nearby player</dd><dt>Cut</dt><dd>An offensive movement used to get away from the defender, often characterized by at least one sharp change of direction.</dd><dt>Defender</dt><dd>Any of the six players playing defense not covering the thrower</dd><dt>Downfield</dt><dd>The part of the playing field in the direction of the offensive team’s endzone relative to the thrower.</dd><dt>Dump or Reset Pass</dt><dd>A short lateral or backwards pass. A pass not necessarily intended to gain yards, but to move the disc to a better position on the field, or reset the stall count.</dd><dt>Dump/Reset</dt><dd>An offensive player that sets up laterally or upfield of the player with the disc, with the purpose of getting the disc in a better position to throw downfield and gain yards</dd><dt>Fake</dt><dd>A motion used by the thrower to make the marker believe that s/he intends to throw the disc in one direction (or from one release point), when, in fact, s/he plans to throw the disc in another direction.</dd><dt>Field Sense</dt><dd>The ability to perceive where one is on the playing field relative to all other players and field boundaries.</dd><dt>Force</dt><dd>The side of the field that the marker is trying to get the thrower to throw to. A marker does this by positioning himself on the opposite side.</dd><dt>Foul</dt><dd>Bodily contact by defensive players, usually to the hands or arms of offensive players, which clearly interferes with either the throw or the catch. Offensive players can also commit fouls. See rules for details.</dd><dt>Leading Pass</dt><dd>A pass which is thrown in front of a receiver in motion, taking into account her/his speed and direction.</dd><dt>Marker</dt><dd>The defensive player who is guarding the thrower. The marker must be positioned at least one disc’s diameter (approximately one foot) from the thrower and cannot straddle the thrower’s pivot foot (see the Rules of Ultimate).</dd><dt>Offsides</dt><dd>Crossing the goal line by any offensive or defensive player prior to the pull.</dd><dt>Pick</dt><dd>A violation that occurs when a defensive player cannot cover her/his opponent because of bodily interference by another player. Generally, it occurs when the offensive player runs so close to another player on either team that the defensive player must stop, slow down, or alter her/his course in order to avoid a collision, thus leaving the offensive player open to receive a pass. Although legal in basketball, it is a dangerous play in Ultimate because of the speed with which players are running.</dd><dt>Pivot</dt><dd>A technique used by the thrower whereby s/he rotates on the ball or toes of the pivot foot in order to change body position for greater throwing range of motion. Pivot foot – The foot upon which the thrower pivots. Usually, it is the opposite foot of the throwing hand.</dd><dt>Pull</dt><dd>Each point begins with the defensive team throwing the disc to the offensive team. The throw is made by a defensive player standing on or behind the endzone line. A pull is similar to a kick off in American football.</dd><dt>Receiver</dt><dd>Any of the six offensive players in position to receive the pass from the thrower.</dd><dt>Spirit of the Game</dt><dd>The underlying concept of fair play inherent in all sports, but made explicit in the rules of Ultimate. For complete text, see the introduction to the Rules of Ultimate.</dd><dt>Stall</dt><dd>A situation when the marker reaches the end of the stall count (ten) before the offensive player has released the disc.</dd><dt>Stall Count</dt><dd>The amount of time the offensive player with the disc has to throw. The stall count must be called aloud by the marker beginning with “stalling, one, two, three…” at one second intervals, being careful not to speed up towards the end of the count.</dd><dt>Strip</dt><dd>When the disc is forcibly knocked from the offensive player’s hands. The disc is returned to the offensive player’s possession and play is initiated with a check.</dd><dt>Travel</dt><dd>An offensive advantage gained by the thrower when s/he takes a step, accelerates or changes directions after a catch and before throwing, or picks up or drags her/his pivot foot during the throw. The disc is brought back to the thrower for a replay, except in the case where the throw resulted in a turnover.</dd><dt>Upfield</dt><dd>The part of the playing field in the opposite direction of the offensive team’s endzone relative to the thrower.</dd></dl>"}, {"slug": "rosters", "title": "Team Rosters & Accolades", "hero": "2ae044147847677213f01d945006dc4a", "html": "<p class=\"lede\">Teams that have represented Cranfield, with captains and awards.</p><p class=\"muted\">(C) captain · (VC) vice-captain · (SC) spirit captain · MVP most valuable player · MIP most improved player · MSP most spirited player</p><h2>2024/2025 Season</h2><div class=\"roster\">\n<div class=\"team\"><figure><img src=\"img/10c90e0e7b7ad797ce4219c2344559c2.webp\" alt=\"University Mixed Indoor Regionals\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><h3>University Mixed Indoor Regionals</h3><ul><li>Huw Whitworth (C)</li><li>Divyansh Sachan</li><li>Shaun Flippance</li><li>Claudio Ferrara</li><li>Chelsea Hua</li><li>Milan Liu</li><li>Meng-Chin Tsai</li></ul></div>\n</div><h2>2023/24 Season</h2><div class=\"roster\">\n<div class=\"team\"><figure><img src=\"img/015c98a3035c0f05ebdb1f2f62eb476b.webp\" alt=\"University Mixed Outdoor Nationals\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><h3>University Mixed Outdoor Nationals</h3><ul><li>Huw Whitworth (SC)</li><li>Divyansh Sachan (C) (MSP)</li><li>Alexis Ropartz (VC)</li><li>Shaun Flippance (MIP-M)</li><li>Erik Nielsen (MVP-M)</li><li>Benoit Said</li><li>Thanavin Mansakul</li><li>Qingqing Hua</li><li>Minrui Ling</li><li>Milan Liu</li><li>Meng-Chin Tsai (MVP-F)</li><li>Xinting Tang (MIP-F)</li><li>Yi Huang</li><li>Ruifan Liu</li><li>Rechuda Kallaka</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"team\"><figure><img src=\"img/24a7c05e231ebf6e07c9edd36dde8831.webp\" alt=\"Cranfield vs Kent (Friendly)\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><h3>Cranfield vs Kent (Friendly)</h3><ul><li>Divyansh Sachan</li><li>Shaun Flippance</li><li>Erik Nielsen</li><li>Qingqing Hua</li><li>Minrui Ling</li><li>Meng-Chin Tsai</li><li>Ruifan Liu</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"team\"><figure><img src=\"img/04605d65ca1d107daa9da3ab5afe9399.webp\" alt=\"Aye-Aye Open 2024\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><h3>Aye-Aye Open 2024</h3><ul><li>Alexis Ropartz (C)</li><li>Divyansh Sachan</li><li>HaoLong Liu</li><li>Shaun Flippance</li><li>Shouqi Shen</li><li>Tongge liu</li><li>Yifan Yang</li><li>Milan Liu (C)</li><li>Qingqing Hua</li><li>Minrui Ling</li><li>Xinting Tang</li><li>Yi Huang</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"team\"><figure><img src=\"img/86f67882b019a30a159e6c7b41e171f5.webp\" alt=\"Bangor BC 2024 (Team A)\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><h3>Bangor BC 2024 (Team A)</h3><ul><li>Shaun Flippance</li><li>Alexis Ropartz</li><li>Tongge Liu</li><li>Qingqing Hua</li><li>Xinting Tang</li><li>Meng-Chin Tsai</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"team\"><figure><img src=\"img/3bcd17e938f69f41fbbefc0bea52abc2.webp\" alt=\"Bangor BC 2024 (Team B)\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><h3>Bangor BC 2024 (Team B)</h3><ul><li>Divyansh Sachan (C)</li><li>Shouqi Shen</li><li>HaoLong Liu</li><li>Thanavin Mansakul</li><li>Rechuda Kallaka</li><li>Yi Huang</li><li>Haijie Li</li><li>Minrui Ling</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"team\"><figure><img src=\"img/29c656adbac4b1333c8d6790e119f742.webp\" alt=\"Bangor Gentley 2023\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><h3>Bangor Gentley 2023</h3><div class=\"result\">10th</div><ul><li>Huw Whitworth (C)</li><li>Divyansh Sachan (C)</li><li>Pablo Jimenez : MIP (M)</li><li>Alexis Ropartz MVP (M)</li><li>Shouqi Shen</li><li>Tongge Liu</li><li>Qingqing Hua : MVP (W)</li><li>Haijie Li</li><li>Yi Huang : MIP (W)</li><li>Xinting Tang</li><li>Minrui Ling</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"team\"><figure><img src=\"img/b6f5f345c1839228ddbbb5521b445642.webp\" alt=\"University Mixed Indoor Regionals 2023 (Team A)\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><h3>University Mixed Indoor Regionals 2023 (Team A)</h3><div class=\"result\">5th</div><ul><li>Divyansh Sachan (C)</li><li>Huw Whitworth (C) (SC)</li><li>Gregor Arbuckle</li><li>Erik Nielsen</li><li>Alexis Ropartz</li><li>Chelsea Hua</li><li>Meng-Chin Tsai</li><li>Laura Barajas</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"team\"><figure><img src=\"img/86215b3a6808e911544e27a1f38377ad.webp\" alt=\"University Mixed Indoor Regionals 2023 (Team B)\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><h3>University Mixed Indoor Regionals 2023 (Team B)</h3><div class=\"result\">13th</div><ul><li>Ruifan Liu (C)</li><li>Minrui Ling</li><li>Rechuda Kallaka</li><li>Haijie Li</li><li>Yi Huang</li><li>Xinting Tang</li><li>Emmanuel Welbeck</li><li>Ravi Parihar</li><li>Shouqi Shen (C)</li><li>Chris Conrad (SC)</li><li>Pablo Jimenez</li><li>Tongge Liu</li></ul></div>\n</div><h2>2022/23 Season</h2><div class=\"roster\">\n<div class=\"team\"><figure><img src=\"img/98a93bd5ee4eb00dda1637cb32da7c00.webp\" alt=\"University Mixed Outdoor Nationals 2023\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><h3>University Mixed Outdoor Nationals 2023</h3><div class=\"result\">21st (+11 places from seed)</div><ul><li>Divyansh Sachan (C)</li><li>Huw Whitworth (C) (MVP)</li><li>Yash Bagrecha</li><li>Chris Conrad</li><li>Doug Faithfull (S.C) (MVP)</li><li>Gregor Arbuckle</li><li>Shouqi Shen</li><li>Minrui Ling</li><li>Ruifan Liu</li><li>Susie Hou</li><li>Chelsea Hua (MVP Women)</li><li>Jiamin Pan</li><li>Haijie Li</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"team\"><figure><img src=\"img/61299a5f08cc5f30f30686ba3dfe7c9a.webp\" alt=\"Aye Aye Open 2023\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><h3>Aye Aye Open 2023</h3><div class=\"result\">11th (Plate Winners)</div><ul><li>Huw Whitworth (C) (MVP)</li><li>Gregor Arbuckle (C)</li><li>Yash Bagrecha</li><li>Shuai Ho</li><li>Shouqi Shen</li><li>Clement Pecheux</li><li>Minrui Ling (MIP)</li><li>Chelsea Hua</li><li>Ruifan Liu</li></ul></div></div>"}, {"slug": "kit", "title": "Kit", "hero": "eb01343f1b2674bac551839f6daadc6d", "html": "<p>Hi!</p>\n<p>Please find below the kit designs for Cranfield Ultimate for 2023 and beyond. We do kit orders twice a year once in October/November and once in January/Feb time. If you have any questions about kit, numbers or any other aspects of the uniform please drop us a message :) Please find the .AI files for current and historic kits below. If you decide to use them, don't forget to cite us! ;P</p>\n<figure><img src=\"img/a35eb3dec89fcd5534db6d09d320820e.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<figure><img src=\"img/cdf4d58a2f77abc9893b4c01bb539287.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Design files</h2><p>Adobe Illustrator (.ai) templates for the current and previous kits.</p><div class=\"kitgrid\"><div class=\"kitcard\"><figure><img src=\"kit/new-2023-24-dark-top-v2.webp\" alt=\"2023/24 dark top kit design\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div class=\"body\"><h4>2023/24 dark top</h4><a class=\"btn-link\" data-kit=\"new-2023-24-dark-top-v2\" href=\"kit/new-2023-24-dark-top-v2.ai\" download>Download .ai</a></div></div><div class=\"kitcard\"><figure><img src=\"kit/new-2023-24-light-top-v2.webp\" alt=\"2023/24 light top kit design\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div class=\"body\"><h4>2023/24 light top</h4><a class=\"btn-link\" data-kit=\"new-2023-24-light-top-v2\" href=\"kit/new-2023-24-light-top-v2.ai\" download>Download .ai</a></div></div><div class=\"kitcard\"><figure><img src=\"kit/short_sleeve_dark_color.webp\" alt=\"Short sleeve, dark kit design\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div class=\"body\"><h4>Short sleeve, dark</h4><a class=\"btn-link\" data-kit=\"short_sleeve_dark_color\" href=\"kit/short_sleeve_dark_color.ai\" download>Download .ai</a></div></div><div class=\"kitcard\"><figure><img src=\"kit/short_sleeve_light_color.webp\" alt=\"Short sleeve, light kit design\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div class=\"body\"><h4>Short sleeve, light</h4><a class=\"btn-link\" data-kit=\"short_sleeve_light_color\" href=\"kit/short_sleeve_light_color.ai\" download>Download .ai</a></div></div></div>"}, {"slug": "throwing", "title": "Throwing Techniques", "hero": "83e19a6f272fd6f680f59a0cfc92ab0e", "html": "<ul><li>Backhand</li><li>Forehand</li><li>Overheads</li></ul>\n<p>Welcome to a resource for learning Basic Throws in ultimate frisbee. These pages are dedicated to helping you develop essential throwing skills, covering the basics of forehand, backhand, hammer, and more. Whether you're a beginner looking to grasp the fundamentals or an experienced player aiming to refine your technique, this guide is designed to be clear and to the point. Let's dive in and elevate your throwing game to new heights.</p><div class=\"cards\"><a class=\"card\" href=\"#backhand\"><h3>Backhand</h3><p>Stance, grip and throwing motion.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#forehand\"><h3>Forehand</h3><p>The flick: grip, wrist snap and release.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#overheads\"><h3>Overheads (Hammer)</h3><p>Hammers over the mark.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#catching\"><h3>Catching</h3><p>Clap, rim and one-handed catches, then receiving on the move.</p></a></div>"}, {"slug": "backhand", "title": "Backhand", "hero": "1af3fe5350952f6c8d0aedc56ddd9989", "html": "<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"As1X0JNWiLY\"></div>\n<p>Backhand Throw</p>\n<p>The backhand throw is by far the most frequently played technique in Frisbee throwing by beginners. It is no wonder that it is one of the first basics in school and club training because it is so easy to learn and can be used in so many ways.</p>\n<p>Beachhead: Stand sideways to the target and make sure the right foot is further forward. Bend the knees a little and look over the shoulder of the throwing arm.</p>\n<p>Grip: Place your thumb on the top of the target and grip the target from below with your other fingers. If you like, your index finger stretched on the outer edge of the target can help to stabilize the target. The palm of your hand lying there also contributes to this. The ideal stance is when there is a pistol-like 45-degree angle to the ground.</p>\n<p>Throwing motion: First, bring your throwing arm back towards your hip. Bend your wrist so that the inside of your forearm and the disc touch. When you start to gain momentum, you will notice that your upper body rotates slightly and the throw starts with the upper body continuing to rotate upwards. Open your elbow and stretch your arm. At the same time your wrist stretches (as quickly as possible!), which provides the last bit of speed. In the course of the throwing movement, shift your weight from your back leg to your front leg. If everything goes well, your hand will point in the direction of the target after the disc has been thrown.</p>"}, {"slug": "forehand", "title": "Forehand", "hero": "1e92ccacc42779229e566d019fa5bfb7", "html": "<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"AiU5518a1q0\"></div>\n<p>Forehand Throw</p>\n<p>A little more difficult than the backhand throw, but not to be underestimated, especially for long throws!</p>\n<p>Beachhead: You stand with your legs slightly apart and your left foot further forward. Your hips and knees are slightly bent. You look over your left shoulder towards your target and hold the disc at stomach level next to your body.</p>\n<p>Grip: Your thumb holds the disc from above while your index finger points to the centre of the disc and your middle finger presses against the inside of the disc. You have tightened your ring and little finger.</p>\n<p>Throwing motion: First, lunge slightly backwards, letting your wrist bend outwards. Now bring your elbow forward as close as possible to your body. Make sure that your forearm is at about the same level as your hip when it passes your elbow. As soon as this is the case, snap your wrist forward. Important: The harder your middle finger presses the inner edge of the disc, the higher the rotation will ultimately be. Release the disc when your arm is almost fully extended and pointing outwards.</p>"}, {"slug": "overheads", "title": "Overheads (Hammer)", "hero": "e15f6e600b8dfc624b370e587d685a2e", "html": "<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"NbwitQD0ATQ\"></div>\n<p>Very useful when the time is right. The hammer throw can be lofted over the defense and landed directly onto the intended teammate. It is also good when someone is marking you hard and nearing the end of the stall count. You can easily throw is over the marker if need be. To throw a hammer (righty) simply pretend like you are throwing an axe. First, hold the disc with your pointer and middle finger on the inner side of the disc, your thumb on the other side. (It is typically held the same way as a Flick.</p>\n<p>The top of the disc should be facing your left side. Tilt the top of the disc slightly to the left side to give it an angle. Pull back and release the disc when it gets even with your forehead, keep your elbow bent at all times. If done right the disc should start out tilted and slowly turn upside-down.</p>\n<p>The disc should land completely upside down on its face because it makes it more predictable and easier to catch for you teammate. If the disc corkscrews then you are probably throwing at too much of an angle for for that particular distance. You should use less tilt for far ranges and more for short ranges. Good for all ranges but works best at medium-range.</p>"}, {"slug": "offence", "title": "Offensive Strategy", "hero": "e3ed86cc3649f924db618724f23d8efd", "html": "<p>Welcome to a comprehensive guide on Basic Offensive Strategy. In the following pages, we'll delve into essential tactics such as Vert Stack, ISO, and Endzone plays. This concise resource is designed to provide you with straightforward insights into these fundamental offensive strategies, empowering you to enhance your understanding and performance on the field.</p>\n<p>Something that is applicable for both indoor and outdoor frisbee is the concept of phases:</p>\n<figure><img src=\"img/9ca72ca6058928337b5ae9397fce3be8.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p>Phase 1 occurs once we have directly caught the disc and look for an immediate continuation upfield either deep cut or a slash, if there is no option we throw a fake and move on to phase 2.</p>\n<p>Phase 2 looks for an upfield cut from the stack, if this is not viable the handler fakes again and moves in to the rest phase (Phase 3). It is important that Phase 3 is NEVER shorter than 5 seconds however the other two phases can be adjusted as shown below.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"img/a5b04887d891d0202031adb86cc3d703.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p>Here we see that the phase 1 has been shortened to one second and phase 2 time frame has been increased. In some instances however we might not want a phase 2 and if there is no direct continuation look for the rest directly.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"img/b30468b618cc212927c08873e7070df8.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div class=\"cards\"><a class=\"card\" href=\"#indoors\"><h3>Indoor Offence</h3><p>ISO and &quot;two in&quot; end zone plays.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#outdoors\"><h3>Outdoor Offence</h3><p>Our outdoor playbook.</p></a></div><div class=\"cards\"><a class=\"card\" href=\"#cutting-basics\"><h3>Cutting Basics</h3><p>Going deep, coming under and timing the next cut.</p></a></div>"}, {"slug": "indoors", "title": "Indoor Offence", "hero": "e3ed86cc3649f924db618724f23d8efd", "html": "<p>In this segment, we focus on the strategies and formations used in indoor ultimate frisbee, particularly in the endzone. The endzone structure involves organizing players to maximize scoring opportunities in the limited space available. Effective endzone strategies require precise timing and coordination to create clear passing lanes and high-percentage scoring chances. One key tactic is the Isolation (ISO) play, where a single offensive player is isolated against a defender, allowing for a one-on-one matchup. This approach leverages the agility and quick decision-making of the offensive player to exploit defensive weaknesses and score efficiently.</p><div class=\"cards\"><a class=\"card\" href=\"#iso\"><h3>ISO (Indoors)</h3><p>Isolation play and the handler weave.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#endzone-indoors\"><h3>End Zone (Indoors)</h3><p>The &quot;two in&quot; end zone set.</p></a></div>"}, {"slug": "iso", "title": "ISO (Indoors)", "hero": "cd22b765b01d0be8c7633a2b4e402db9", "html": "<h2>Structure</h2>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/05cf460128f33b74cdcbb098fe380372.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>Iso is an alternative indoors strategy to vert stack. We set up with 2 handlers, the iso alone in the centre, and the other 2 players go straight to the end zone.</p></div></div>\n<h2>ISO</h2>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/6a15cc3fd3a51cabcfdf4d220bfb426c.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 1</h4><p>This play is handler initiated. This means that the iso should not move until the disc is thrown. The thrower should float the disc into a space that the iso will get to before their defender.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/02d84d8bac621566e3df4f3758c1ce42.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 2</h4><p>If we aren't close to the end zone yet, we set up another iso and exactly the same process is used.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/6c65a43c6a10bdbb134ac0ed1b85031d.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 3</h4><p>Once we are getting close to the end zone, we will initiate the \"two in\" end zone play</p></div></div></div>\n<h2>Handler Weave</h2>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/149dbc81d7ad955d12b06f23dc6bd67f.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 1</h4><p>If the handler is not confident in the iso throw, they should immediately look to the dump. As always with resets, if the throw to the dump is open, then it should be thrown.</p>\n<p>If not, the handler fakes at the dump and they cut up the line. Importantly, the iso should cut towards the far sideline at this point. This is crucial to stop the defender poaching off the iso.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/a8c38c7353a14544058417e0665936cb.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 2</h4><p>Next, just as in a vert stack reset, the iso should then cut back towards the dump space to make an option in case the throw up the line is marked out.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/0b716c88ca9142f2556528b4881c7221.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 3</h4><p>If this swing is also marked out, our next option is to look back to the dump, who should now be bouncing across the pitch horizontally. This step is a really powerful option, but is often forgotten.</p></div></div></div>"}, {"slug": "endzone-indoors", "title": "End Zone (Indoors)", "hero": "ba70d4bfa6fe0d4fea57f69525c5fb16", "html": "<p>Indoors</p>\n<p>Indoors we will hopefully manage to score from further away most of the time, or in flow so that the defence doesn't have time to set. But if we are slowing down near the end zone, we will usually call \"two in\". Here's how that should work.</p>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/9d477f89b883a41c36a1004a038925b7.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>Two players set up in the centre of the end zone. The defence will probably set up a clamp, as shown.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/32d134385258ea8d86da34f6d88ee184.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>The two players should attempt to get free in opposite directions. This will usually be initiated by the cutter at the front, so that the second cutter can see which way they go and go the other way.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/0229760a300e6edcb4b95ed4247fd883.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>If they fail to get free, one of the cutters should leave the end zone and the other should cut back across to try and get free.</p></div></div>"}, {"slug": "outdoors", "title": "Outdoor Offence", "hero": "3ee335dffe7d0f1f1c35b1fb6b4365be", "html": "<ul><li>Vert Stack</li><li>Endzone</li><li>Anti-Zone</li><li>Ho Stack</li><li>Weaves</li></ul>\n<p>In this segment, we explore various methods to enhance outdoor ultimate frisbee offensive play. We delve into the vertical stack, a formation that creates space and organized cutting lanes for effective disc movement. The outdoor endzone offense focuses on positioning and timing to maximize scoring opportunities within the endzone. Overcoming a zone defense requires strategic disc movement and player positioning to find gaps and exploit weaknesses. The horizontal stack offers an alternative offensive structure, spreading players across the field to create more lateral movement and dynamic cutting options. Additionally, we cover the outdoor handler weave, a technique where handlers exchange quick passes to advance the disc while maintaining possession and control.</p><h2>The playbook</h2><p>Here we have a basis for our outdoor playbook, covering vert stack, HO stack, handler moment, end zone players and out counter zone strategies in addition to slightly more complex strats such as our side-stack structure. Have a look over it, learn it, use it :)</p>\n<ul><li>Vert Stack</li><li>HO Stack</li><li>Handler Movement</li><li>End Zone</li><li>Counter-Zone</li><li>Side Stack</li></ul><div class=\"cards\"><a class=\"card\" href=\"#vert-stack\"><h3>Vert Stack</h3><p>Structure, cuts, resets and continuations.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#ho-stack\"><h3>Horizontal Stack</h3><p>Horo set-up, cutting pattern and resets.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#side-stack\"><h3>Side Stack</h3><p>One isolated cutter at a time, clear out, next from the back.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#handler-movement\"><h3>Handler Movement</h3><p>Set plays 1–4 off the sideline.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#endzone-outdoors\"><h3>End Zone (Outdoors)</h3><p>Plays A, B and C: strike, ISO and vert.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#beating-zones\"><h3>Beating a Zone</h3><p>Around, through and inside the zone.</p></a></div>"}, {"slug": "vert-stack", "title": "Vert Stack", "hero": "3ee335dffe7d0f1f1c35b1fb6b4365be", "html": "<nav class=\"toc\"><b>On this page</b><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Structure\">Structure</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Under Cuts\">Under Cuts</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Deep Cuts\">Deep Cuts</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Handler Resets\">Handler Resets</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Continuations\">Continuations</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"How to know if you&#x27;re doing it wrong!\">How to know if you&#x27;re doing it wrong!</button></nav>\n<h2>Structure</h2>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/660c4f63cbef9c9c275f608d85fee866.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>Our vert stack should be set up something like this, with 2 handlers back and everyone else in the stack. The person at the back of the stack is always the first to cut.</p>\n<p>Important notes on stack positioning:</p>\n<p>Players in the stack should be at least 1-2 metres away from each other, so that they have space to cut</p>\n<p>There should be enough space between the front of stack and the handlers for a cutter to come across that space</p>\n<p>Make sure the stack does not drift over towards the side of the pitch where the disc is, as this decreases cutting space</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/053ba951ab094fc68dd2d6ded07b0417.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>If the dump is on the open side, they should be positioned slightly further back.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/2f80fcb24a033fe2c6c132182dfbdf32.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>If the dump is on the break side, they should be level with the other handler.</p></div></div></div>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/36b96cd6513bf5c9309becd7f308f759.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Under Cuts</h4><p>Basic under cuts should look something like this. You should always cut under diagonally (see cut 1). If you do not get the disc, you should clear out either across the front of the stack (a \"7 cut\") or go straight deep. Cuts labelled \"2\" in this diagram should always be sprinted, and you should still be looking to receive the disc.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/f9f17268de04f547e63f32ae31a11ea0.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Deep Cuts</h4><p>Deep cuts will look something like this. When you are cutting deep it should always be vertically up the pitch, not diagonally. This gives the thrower more space to throw into.</p></div></div></div>\n<h2>Handler Resets</h2>\n<p>Our handler resets this year will be very similar for both open and break side, but the initial cuts may look slightly different due to the starting position of the dump. For that reason, there are two images for each step of the explanation.</p>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/4d717633995484736e37786d68dc3291.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><h3>Step 1</h3>\n<p>Once the handler looks at the dump, they have initiated the reset. No more cuts should come from the back of the stack. The first option is a space throw for the dump to catch. Remember to always throw this if it's on!</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/050d8cc795d6871088a3105c52521bb8.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><h3>Step 2</h3>\n<p>If the space throw is not on, the handler should fake a throw at the dump. This is the trigger for the dump to make a cut diagonally up the line.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/afaa7b4d504dd3dfc4f9752c5e2a5b92.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><h3>Step 3</h3>\n<p>If they do not get free, the handler's next option is the front of stack. When the dump is cutting up the line, the front of stack should cut back towards the dump position to look for a pass.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/4110610cd6bcc5e14bb4a70130d625a2.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><h3>Step 4</h3>\n<p>If that still hasn't worked, then to be honest it's probably too late. But if it's somehow not too late, the handler fakes at the front of stack, at which point they turn and cut up the line. (It's probably gonna be a stall out, just huck it).</p></div></div>\n<h2>Continuations</h2>\n<h3>From the back:</h3>\n<p>In order to keep our offense flowing as quickly as possible, cutters need to be ready for a pass to be completed. For example, if the first cutter gets the disc under, the second cutter should already have been driving deep so that they have more space to cut under into, as shown below.</p>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/d3769179e8ca738d81883ecc1c8b7d1b.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><h3>Summary</h3>\n<ul><li>When? - You are second in the stack and the first cutter is going under</li><li>What? - You should drive deep to set up your cut</li><li>Why? - It creates more space to cut under and allows us to gain more yards</li></ul></div></div>\n<h3>From the front</h3>\n<p>This continuation is important because fast disc movement from one side of the pitch to the other can be an excellent way of getting around the defense. If the disc is swung from the side into the centre, the next cut should come from the front of the stack to continue the disc's movement across the pitch. Cuts will then come from the back again.</p>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/73212104dc3ed9deaa01f80208d05894.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><h3>Summary</h3>\n<ul><li>When? - You are front of stack and the disc is being swung into the centre</li><li>What? - Cut out wide to continue the disc's path across the pitch</li><li>Why? - It allows us to quickly change which side of the pitch we are attacking</li></ul></div></div>\n<h3>Handler Strike</h3>\n<p>As far as I'm aware we've never really officially taught this one, but it's a very useful move so I think we should add it to our arsenal. This continuation is similar to doing an immediate handler reset, but without the space throw option.</p>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/ef610a42383a6f6e81960caa4e3f4192.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><h3>Summary</h3>\n<p>When? - You had the disc in a central location and you have passed it out wide to the dump</p>\n<p>What? - Immediately cut up the line to get a return pass. The front of stack should cut back to where you were</p>\n<p>Why? - It allows us to either gain yards or get the disc back into the centre</p></div></div>\n<h2>How to know if you&#x27;re doing it wrong!</h2>\n<h3>Cutting Too Late</h3>\n<p>If you’re in the back of the stack, you’ll know that you took too long to cut if you see an experienced player busting out of the middle of the stack and cutting you off. Experienced players or captains will often see that the stack has stopped flowing, and take it upon themselves to right the ship. You may have been just about to make your cut when this happens, and get discouraged that you got cut off. Next time, anticipate when it will be your turn, and start up a little earlier.</p>\n<h3>Cutting Too Early</h3>\n<p>If you make a cut when you’re not at the back of the stack, you’ll likely cut off the initiator, as they make their move from the back of the stack. There will be groaning – you’ve just brought your defender into the throwing lane so that you could try a cut in limited space that would net fewer upfield yards. Get back in the stack and wait for it to be your turn.</p>\n<h3>Being Too Stagnant</h3>\n<p>If you’re in the middle of the stack, but your defender manages to get a poach block on an under cut, that’s on you. You were too complacent, letting your defender take advantage of you and jump into the throwing lane. Just because you’re in the middle of the stack doesn’t mean that you’ll never cut – just having the aura of someone who’s looking for every opportunity to get open can keep your defender on their toes and close to you.</p>\n<h3>Resources:</h3>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"Tg3F4Zp0K4I\"></div>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"QmZWv0bM9yM\"></div>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"BKKQvP_CHOA\"></div>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"-Shh0MwXtqY\"></div>\n<h2>Six-cutter stacks</h2>\n<p>Some teams play one handler with six cutters in the stack instead of two handlers and five cutters, which makes everyone a possible handler.</p>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"vs-central\"></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cut from the ends, clear to the middle.</strong> First cuts come from the back (C6) or the front (C1); cutters clear into the middle (C3–C4), so the sequence keeps repeating. If nothing goes downfield, the dump space is still clear for a reset.</li>\n<li><strong>Who goes first depends on field position.</strong> Far from the end zone there's room downfield, so the back of the stack goes first. As you get closer, the front of the stack (up the line) uses the space better.</li>\n<li><strong>The front two act as handlers,</strong> swinging and resetting; the front player also keeps the stack close to the disc and calls where it should be.</li>\n<li>Read the players next to you, especially the back two and the front two, and call the stack's new position whenever the disc changes side.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Side-stack pull play</h3>\n<p>A one-off play when you receive the pull: the nearest cutter swings it from the handler; the middle cutters clear under to open the field while the second-from-back fakes deep; then that player comes towards the disc and the last player goes deep to finish. If it doesn't work, rebuild the stack and play normal flow.</p>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"vs-pull-play\"></div>\n<p>More stack drills: <a href=\"#drill-vert-give-go-deep\">Give-and-Go to the Deep Shot</a>, <a href=\"#drill-vert-circuit\">the Central Stack Circuit</a> and <a href=\"#drill-kitkat\">KitKat</a>.</p>\n"}, {"slug": "ho-stack", "title": "Horizontal Stack", "hero": "3ee335dffe7d0f1f1c35b1fb6b4365be", "html": "<nav class=\"toc\"><b>On this page</b><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Structure\">Structure</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"The Cutting Pattern\">The Cutting Pattern</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Handler Resets\">Handler Resets</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Moving up pitch\">Moving up pitch</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Further Resources\">Further Resources</button></nav>\n<h2>Structure</h2>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/0a46ea7a98008ad07da66173bad3d891.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>Horo is set up with three handlers and 4 cutters spread across the pitch. This year we will be using the horo system that Lemmings came up with last year. This system is all about swinging the disc and creating isolated cutting situations. More on that below.</p>\n<p>The cutting positions are referred to using the numbers 1 to 4 depending on where the disc is. If the disc is on the right, then the cutter that is furthest right is \"1\" and the cutter furthest left is \"4\".</p></div></div>\n<h2>The Cutting Pattern</h2>\n<h3>When the wide handlers have the disc:</h3>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/cc062514cf13ce9ac5f187a5f0e928e8.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>We should set up like this, with cutters 2 and 3 allowing cutter 1 to have lots of space. Cutter 1 should be decisive and either go under or deep.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/66d748a1a81aee07039a6fbf73fe8220.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>If cutter 1 does not get free, they should clear out by going across into position 3. They should not come back under after going deep or vice versa. Also note that on the under cut you are still an option when initially clearing out, much like doing a 7 cut from a vert stack.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/73f67fb8327de264f447fea767573317.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>As they are clearing out, cutter 2 should have initiated their cut by coming horizontally across, and they should now quickly change direction to come under or go deep.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/ad2222137c36a6bc6943de336a24ae65.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>If they don't get the disc, the pattern just continues until the handler reset is initiated.</p></div></div></div>\n<h3>When the disc is in the middle:</h3>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/4578031dbc6f0f93ffa415642032fb30.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>If the disc is central, the handler should ideally loo to swing it to one of the other handlers. However, if this option is not on immediately then they should look upfield to the middle two cutters. These two cutters should work in tandem, one going under and the other going deep, to try and get the disc. If they don't get the disc, the same two cutters should keep rotating round and do the opposite of what they just did.</p></div></div>\n<h2>Handler Resets</h2>\n<h3>Handler Positioning</h3>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/a9661559f0ee7bb308f15f0788f5ae9f.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>The image on the left shows a situation in which the handler 1 might initiate the handler reset, by looking at handler 2. In this horo system, the handlers should always set up as shown. In particular, notice that handler 3 is pushed quite far forward.</p>\n<p>When the handler reset is initiated, the next cutter should not move across as they usually would to start their cut. Instead, they should leave a large amount of space for the handlers to use up the line.</p></div></div>\n<h3>The Reset Pattern</h3>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/e9d09070ba10eb08ad55ac34a5b3b0a7.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>When handler 1 looks at handler 2, handler 2 should immediately cut backwards in anticipation of a space throw. If they are faked at, they should quickly change direction to cut up the line.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/bbc61864fb2dfcffd783beeefcf6803f.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>If the space throw was thrown, handler 2 should immediately look to swing it again to handler 3. If the second swing is thrown, the cutters should now move across, except the cutter labelled 'C', who will become active.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/93d09d8c34536ca2750f1d76c86aefd3.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>If handler 1 fakes at handler 2, handler 2 cuts up the line. When this happens, the cutters should all start moving up the pitch in anticipation of handler 2 receiving the pass. Handler 3 should start cutting horizontally across the pitch in case they are needed.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/9a936f3f4e13934b8b9cd67e5613db01.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>If it is thrown up the line, the nearest cutter should be ready to become active. The other handlers reposition themselves and we end up back in our usual set up.</p></div></div></div>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/2c0d9c7ecffd8530655e9a629f438f98.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>If the throw does not go up the line, handler 3 should change direction and cut under to receive a swing. Handler 2 continues to clear out deep and cutter 3 comes back as a third handler.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/89bd0fcca3e4f530f1cacaaba6e33452.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>Much like step 1b, handler 3 should immediately look to swing the disc again to keep the momentum going.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/0d4b06b9c578ecdf1724959667e4d44a.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>Just as with handler 2, handler 3 should cut up the line if they are faked at, and the pattern continues.</p></div></div></div>\n<h2>Moving up pitch</h2>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/0d6f07a2cb4f288a3e50667d3c2e128c.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>If a pass is completed to a cutter coming under, we need to know how to rotate such that we can keep our system going. Let's take a look at the situation shown in the image on the right.</p>\n<p>When the cutter receives the disc, they have 2 options. They could either quickly pop it back to the handler who threw it originally, or turn and face upfield to look for cuts. What should happen in each situation is explained below.</p></div></div>\n<h3>Pop Pass</h3>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/a33dbb0bb3252c306a7cfd0629b188ea.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>All players should start moving upfield as the pop pass is thrown, so that they are ready to continue the pattern as soon as the handler regains possession of the disc.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/7841674d38eeea441d2e862218e99d9b.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>The original cutter should clear out in the same way they would have if they had never received the disc, making their way to position 3. The next cutter should start cutting as normal.</p></div></div></div>\n<h3>Looking Upfield</h3>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/09ecc3bead87d357a7b83e91e8dca62b.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>If the original cutter is effectively now a handler. The furthest handler should push through to become a cutter at position 3. The other handlers should rotate around to fill the gaps.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/e592f8b646fb480d941f2a435f41c99a.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>The cutting pattern then continues as normal.</p></div></div></div>\n<h2>Further Resources</h2>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"FTZdFGvHuXs\"></div>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"rGFbIj5iVnA\"></div>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.flikulti.com/theory/horizontal-stack/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.flikulti.com/theory/horizontal-stack/</a></p>\n<h2>Spacing</h2>\n<p>Each cutter has two places to go: the under space towards the handlers and the deep space towards the end zone. Keep cutters about 10–15 m from the handlers.</p>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"ho-spacing\"></div>\n\n<h2>Calling the first cut</h2>\n<p>From a dead disc, the thrower tells the cutters early (before picking up) who goes first. \"Wide\" or \"middle\" picks the active pair; \"home\" or \"away\" picks which of them comes under first, using the cutter nearest the home or away sideline. Then the other goes deep. Defences pick up numbered calls quickly; home/away is harder to read.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Move your defender around before the call, and put a change of direction into the first cut.</li>\n<li>Cut early and hard, and keep cutting for the full ten seconds.</li>\n<li>Avoid crossing the lane on your first cut; go deep only once the lane is clear.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>The cutters who aren't active</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stay wide, level with play and moving,</strong> so your defender can't relax and poach the active pair.</li>\n<li><strong>If your defender poaches,</strong> shout \"POACH!\" and then use it: cut towards the disc without getting in the active cutters' way.</li>\n<li><strong>Deep strike:</strong> when a receiver catches running upfield (the \"power position\"), commit hard to the first few steps of a deep cut. If the space or the throw isn't there, peel back to your spot. Done a few times, it stops your defender poaching.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Handlers don't bust upfield into the cutting space early in the count. Look for the give-and-go at stall zero, then set up as the reset and wait to be engaged. A receiver stays a handler until they push through to the far side as a continuation cutter.</p>\n\n<h2>Set plays for a horizontal stack</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diamond:</strong> the two middle cutters make complementary diamond-shaped cuts, one under while the other goes deep. The two wide cutters keep sliding and faking to stop their defenders poaching, then either join the flow or clear. Keep the disc central and the first throw going towards the middle.</li>\n<li><strong>Diamond push:</strong> when a middle cutter is tired or hasn't got free, they swap with a wide cutter without blocking the lanes, so the diamond can cycle again and again. This copes better with poaches and junk defences than the basic diamond.</li>\n<li><strong>Double piston:</strong> two pairs (C1 with C3, C2 with C4) run straight up-and-down \"tracks\" in opposite directions. When one catches, their partner (moving the same way) sprints deep while the other pair offers two unders. Easy to learn, very aggressive, and tiring: everyone runs at once.</li>\n<li><strong>Sweep cut:</strong> one wide cutter cuts flat across the field at top speed while the other three clear deep with good timing and come back under after the catch, giving three continuation options.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"ho-sweep\"></div>\n<p>See <a href=\"#drill-ho-diamonds-pistons\">Diamonds & Pistons</a>, <a href=\"#drill-ho-initiation-calls\">Home and Away Calls</a>, <a href=\"#drill-ho-walkthrough\">the Ho Walk-Through</a>, <a href=\"#drill-ho-staggered-deep\">Staggered Deep Cuts</a>, <a href=\"#drill-handler-strike\">Handler Strike</a>, <a href=\"#drill-sailboat\">Sailboat</a> and <a href=\"#drill-ho-hucking-circuit\">the Hucking Circuit</a>.</p>\n"}, {"slug": "handler-movement", "title": "Handler Movement", "hero": "3ee335dffe7d0f1f1c35b1fb6b4365be", "html": "<p>The premise of this is that we want to play really really boring frisbee. Handler set plays are crucial for maintaining offensive flow and creating scoring opportunities. These plays utilize speed and agility of handlers to effectively move the disc, break down defenses, and reset the stall count. By coordinating specific movements and passes among handlers, teams can exploit defensive weaknesses, maintain possession, and set up more advantageous field positions. Additionally, well-executed handler set plays can create space for cutters, making it easier to advance the disc downfield and ultimately score.  It's important that downfield cutters do not interfere with handler movement, as this allows handlers to operate freely and create the necessary space for advancing the disc.</p>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/ac23d861d52b0199d50f2f5e7ef95479.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><h3>1</h3>\n<p>In the event that a call of  1 , 11 , 12, 13 ... 19, 1xx is called it is assumed that we are going to play set play 1 to move the disc off the sideline.</p>\n<p>Here the handler on disc (HoD) picks up and instantly swings to the center with little movement from the swing handler.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/2e0ad50157ccfb0c9bb2c666b5266a02.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><h3>2</h3>\n<p>In the event that a call of  2, 20, 2x is called it is assumed that we are going to play set play 2 to move the disc off the sideline.</p>\n<p>Acting as a more traditional method here the dump handler (RHS) makes a sharp cut up the line while the front of stack comes under. This opens up two plausible options for HoD.</p>\n<p>Up the line</p>\n<p>swing to centre</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/819a2894c32685820d1ae3ed66c87e03.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><h3>3</h3>\n<p>In the event that a call of  3, 30, 3x is called it is assumed that we are going to play set play 3 to move the disc off the sideline.</p>\n<p>The swing handler cuts up the line as if looking for a pass than does a 45 Degree turn towards the centre of the pitch for an upfield cross from HoD.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/677910e85c2dc8fe335e7c0dfcd1eb58.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><h3>4</h3>\n<p>In the event that a call of  4, 40, 4x is called it is assumed that we are going to play set play 2 to move the disc.</p>\n<p>The dump handler cuts up the line as if to receive an up the line pass. the handler then cuts back down the line to the original position throwing off their defender and opting for a viable swing option.</p></div></div>\n<h2>Reset principles</h2>\n<p>Whatever set play we run, the same principles keep the disc alive.</p>\n<h3>The thrower</h3>\n<p>Use the first few seconds to look upfield. If nothing safe is on, switch to keeping the disc moving: <strong>turn infield and engage your reset at stall 4</strong>, earlier if you're trapped on the sideline. Turning and making eye contact tells the reset they're now the option. Throw straight away if they're free, or fake to tell them to cut, then trust them to get open. Don't panic and start hunting other options.</p>\n<h3>The reset's priorities</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Keep the stall count low.</li>\n<li>Get the disc off the sideline before a trap is set.</li>\n<li>Attack free space: swings, up-line cuts.</li>\n<li>Keep the disc moving: give-and-goes, dishes.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Where to stand</h3>\n<p>Level with or behind the disc, in the handler space, clear of upfield cuts and throws. Close enough for a lead pass, far enough to have room to cut, with at least two directions to get free. Teammates must keep out of this space too.</p>\n<p>Avoid <strong>no man's land</strong>: the wide-open space behind a thrower on the sideline. You lose yards, you're still on the line with no continuation, and if you don't get it your defender is now blocking the swing.</p>\n<h3>Getting free</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>If you're open already (poached, or with good position), hold your spot, show a hand and wait.</li>\n<li>If not, don't let your defender settle: work round their back so they have to turn their head, use side-steps and sliding, and drift upfield (more room under) or back (more room up the line).</li>\n<li>When the thrower engages you, one change of direction then <strong>commit</strong>: low, explosive steps. Lots of little jukes just help the defender.</li>\n<li>If it doesn't come, turn hard into another space or clear out so someone else can cut.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>The three phases for every handler</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Disc in the air:</strong> move to give an immediate option or get to a support spot.</li>\n<li><strong>Early in the count (0–4):</strong> set up and support without getting in the cutting space while the thrower looks upfield.</li>\n<li><strong>Reset mode (4–10):</strong> actively engage to get the disc moving.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Each time the disc moves: follow it and scan upfield; take a give-and-go or hand-off if it's on (a handler running onto the disc has momentum and vision, the \"power position\"); talk about who is reset, who is third handler and who pushes through; then get into a good reset spot and let the thrower know you're there. If a give-and-go doesn't come immediately, go back to a good set-up position rather than drifting.</p>\n\n<h2>The third handler</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vertical stack:</strong> sets up at the front of the stack in the middle, slightly offset so the break throw across the stack is always on. They also set how deep the stack is.</li>\n<li><strong>Anticipate the reset and swing.</strong> Delay your cut so there's space left to attack on the far side. Rule of thumb: cut for the swing if the reset pass doesn't travel past the stack.</li>\n<li><strong>Back-up reset (\"cycle\"):</strong> if the reset clears without the disc, cut into the handler space to get free.</li>\n<li><strong>Horizontal stack:</strong> starts on the far side, slightly upfield of the disc, ready for the swing. When the reset cuts up the line, drift infield to a front-of-stack spot so you can attack the swing space or cycle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Practise with <a href=\"#drill-reset-and-continue\">Reset and Continue</a>, <a href=\"#drill-third-handler-drill\">the Third Handler Drill</a> and <a href=\"#drill-stall-four-restart\">the Stall-Four Restart Game</a>.</p>\n"}, {"slug": "endzone-outdoors", "title": "End Zone (Outdoors)", "hero": "d4c16fabd91be44272f2af17e9d79116", "html": "<nav class=\"toc\"><b>On this page</b><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Strike\">Strike</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Overview\">Overview</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Isolation (ISO) Play\">Isolation (ISO) Play</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Standard Vert\">Standard Vert</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Resources\">Resources</button></nav>\n<h2>Strike</h2>\n<h3>Overview</h3>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/a9dac56ee78351d71af5a7ae957ff724.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>We will use end zone plays if we are using vert or horo, or if we are using hex but we don't score in flow. We will also use them whenever there is a turnover or a timeout near the end zone. For outdoors this year, we will use the same 3 plays that we usually do: strike, iso, and standard(ish) vert. We will call these using words beginning with A, B, and C, respectively. It is recommended that these 3 options are called according to the diagram on the left. This page will also briefly cover indoors end zone plays at the end.</p></div></div>\n<h2>Strike</h2>\n<p>Play A will be our most commonly used play. Both B and C eventually transition into A, so it's good to know this one. The most important aspect of it is that there are no cuts from the back. It is all strikes and cuts from the front.</p>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/5a981d79a66572ed63422196d59e284a.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 1</h4><p>When Play A is called, the dump should immediately strike up the line (no fake needed). Meanwhile, the front of stack comes back to fill the space.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/f394eae3c8a031a56b582acc5164c6e1.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 2</h4><p>If the pass was not on, the disc should be swung back to the middle.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/e9891eede2e44bada80c454b05561537.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 3</h4><p>We should then immediately look to continue this swing with another cut from the front.</p></div></div></div>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/c938a1b1c795e8e068d6c9c313b927b9.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 4</h4><p>Every time the disc is swung from a central area to a wide area, the thrower should immediately cut up the line.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/d5488f28bbd5dee9983f990ce022a8b4.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 5</h4><p>If it's not on again, we swing it back across. The player that was wide on the other side should come across to the central area for the swing.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/7a7e36f84698ae15a0522a83bed9d7c6.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 6</h4><p>A cut from the front of stack continues the swing and the pattern continues.</p></div></div></div>\n<h3>Isolation (ISO) Play</h3>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/edd949d65bd47134b6cbfd73dced4340.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>For the iso play, one player should set up alone at the front of the stack with the rest of the stack staying out of the way at the back of the end zone. At the start of the move, only the iso should cut.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/32941942a969db55e914645fc8bb77c9.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 1</h4><p>The iso should try to get free with 2 cuts.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/86a227d87af39a8ff3681fc7cced6fb6.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 2</h4><p>If neither of them work, the iso should come out of the end zone. At this moment, a player in the stack may be poached due to 2 people marking the iso. If that is the case, they should cut and make themselves known.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/407dd77135c274ab92bff5d87b0d4559.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 3</h4><p>If this option is not on, the handler should swing to their dump and cut up the line. Once again we are transitioning into Play A.</p></div></div></div>\n<h3>Standard Vert</h3>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/d4a96564cf3235b4a842e37316e5befa.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>This option is similar to a normal vert stack, but it will be a bit more of a set play. As deep options will not be possible, the stack should go all the way to the back of the end zone so that there is as much space as possible to cut into. The set up should look like the diagram on the left. Note: the dump should set up on the open side. Below you can see the steps of how this play works.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/e94e8ed7975df59d4c2e9d49affe38f5.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 1</h4><p>Two players at the back of the stack should cut simultaneously. One to the open side, one to the break side.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/3fa7ab613894ae4010c41eec4e59b2cb.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 2</h4><p>The player on the open side should then do a 7 cut across to the break side. The break side cutter should quickly get back into the stack.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/fb93215dee7008b6362f377a5c35d19a.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><h4>Step 3</h4><p>If none of these options come off, the handler should swing the disc to the dump and immediately cut up the line. At this point we transition into End Zone Play A.</p></div></div></div>\n<h3>Resources</h3>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"Yza3wHWOG5I\"></div>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"CwXH-e84g4I\"></div>\n<h2>More end zone plays</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"#drill-rendezvous-endzone\">Rendezvous</a>: five cutters stacked on the open side all run across the end zone; the called player cuts back to where they started.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#drill-flood-snapback-endzone\">Flood Snap-Back</a>: everyone stops in the middle, the others snap back, and the called player fakes back then breaks to the far side.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#drill-box-cut-iso\">Box-Cut ISO</a>: everyone stays behind the disc while one break-side player goes straight up into the end zone and cuts flat across to the open side.</li>\n</ul>\n"}, {"slug": "beating-zones", "title": "Beating a Zone", "hero": "1d8f869919b170df66b7a8b1be3dea30", "html": "<nav class=\"toc\"><b>On this page</b><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"The &#x27;Around&#x27; Option\">The &#x27;Around&#x27; Option</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Attacking through the Defence\">Attacking through the Defence</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Moving Inside the Front\">Moving Inside the Front</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Resources\">Resources</button></nav>\n<p>At the very basic level there are four key components that are used to break down a zone defence.</p>\n<h2>The &#x27;Around&#x27; Option</h2>\n<p>If you ever played a tournament with us you've 100% heard Huw and Div screaming \"Swing the disc\" on the side-line looking for lateral movement of the disc.</p>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/79fa7f5f42ecb11600def137e42bdaf0.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>If the defense is allowing throws to go from sideline to sideline for any gains, your team should take those throws. These throws are often uncontested, and your team may be able to gain 10-20 yards by simply swinging the disc a few times, as shown below. If the defense does not adjust, you could even work the disc all the way to the endzone without ever throwing a difficult, contested throw.</p></div></div>\n<p>Establish the Around: The offense has gained positive yards by avoiding the defenders and throwing around the zone.</p>\n<h2>Attacking through the Defence</h2>\n<p>The core issue with constantly swinging the disc is that the defence will simply adjust their positioning to stop these lateral moves.  Most likely the defence will spread the front defenders and shown in the Figure below.  These front defenders are now in a position to contest both swing throws from the first diagram, making the around a riskier option. When the front of the zone spreads, this will open up throws through the defense.</p>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/c62b36aa724a85d9605efeb56bd3624f.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>As the front of the zone spreads, several downfield offensive players should assume the role of “poppers,” looking to occupy open alleys of space in between the front and the second level of the defense. After establishing the around, these alleys will become wider, allowing for easier throws through the front.</p></div></div>\n<p>Attacking Through the Defense: As the defensive front spreads out, poppers look to receive through throws in open spaces behind the front.</p>\n<h2>Moving Inside the Front</h2>\n<p>Successfully completing just a few through passes can often move your offense down the length of the field, so defenses are typically quick to make adjustments that deny these throws. One common way defenses adjust is by dropping the front defenders farther away from the disc. This allows them to deny the open spaces that your poppers are looking to exploit, while maintaining the ability to attack wide throws around the defense. Therefore, we now move inside the front.</p>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/ab2bf36030748ba6934eedee10b0dc0a.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>These inside throws (not “inside” breaks!) are short, easy throws from handler to handler that gain positive yards into the space in front of the defence. In addition to easily gaining yards, these inside throws will often shrink the distance between the new thrower and the front of the defence, negating the advantage gained by the sagging defenders, and creating new opportunities for throws through the defence.</p></div></div>\n<p>Moving Inside the Front: As the front sags back, handlers can receive yards inside the defense. This can be coupled with the new thrower looking for a quick through throw to a popper.</p>\n<h2>Resources</h2>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"EVivc0QgPtg\"></div>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"GpDv1pdCIpc\"></div>\n<h2>Six principles against any zone</h2>\n<p>Zones change the space on the field, but they shouldn't change your throwing decisions: no need to start throwing hammers or hitting double-marked players just because it's a zone point.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Always have an easy pass.</strong> One handler stands on the open side as far upfield as they can without being contested, so the count can always be reset.</li>\n<li><strong>Find unmarked space.</strong> Good space is somewhere you're a threat (the thrower can reach you) that moves the disc towards the end zone and away from traps. If you're unmarked in good space, <strong>stand still</strong>: a stationary target is easier to hit.</li>\n<li><strong>Outnumber them.</strong> A 1-3-3 junk puts four around the disc and three behind, so put four behind the wall. Find the weak spot, put a player there, and move again when the defence reacts.</li>\n<li><strong>Advance it around, through, inside or over the wall.</strong> Swings round the wall spread it, which opens holes through it; if it drops back to stop those, attack the space inside; over the top is the most damaging but hardest, especially in wind.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid the trap.</strong> A wide-open pass to the sideline is open because they want you there. If you have to go there, support the new thrower straight away to get it back infield.</li>\n<li><strong>Jailbreak safely.</strong> Once the disc is past the wall, keep it moving with cuts towards the disc and the middle before the zone can reset. But being caught is better than a panicked turnover: if they contain it, bank the yards and set up again.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Handlers should try to receive facing forwards, so they can see upfield and hit the next pass quickly. Practise with <a href=\"#drill-over-the-top-zone-game\">the Over-the-Top Zone Game</a> and <a href=\"#drill-swinging-vs-zone\">Swinging vs Zone</a>.</p>\n"}, {"slug": "defence", "title": "Defensive Strategy", "hero": "d6056998739eb0cea2d27eea522a24a6", "html": "<ul><li>Switching &amp; Surrounding</li><li>House Zone</li><li>Clamp and Flood</li><li>Outdoor Zones</li><li>Boxing Out</li></ul>\n<p>Welcome to a comprehensive guide on Basic Defensive Strategy in ultimate frisbee. The pages ahead are dedicated to equipping you with the knowledge and skills necessary for effective defensive play. We'll explore key strategies, including man-to-man marking, zone defense, and effective handler coverage. Additionally, you'll discover insights into essential defensive techniques such as switching and surrounding, clamp defense, and flood defense. Whether you're new to the game or seeking to sharpen your defensive prowess, this resource is tailored to provide clear and concise insights. Let's embark on a journey to enhance your understanding of defensive tactics, including these advanced techniques, contributing to your success on the ultimate frisbee field.</p>\n<p>This set of pages includes:</p>\n<p>Switching and Surrounding</p>\n<p>House Zone (Indoors)</p>\n<p>Clamp and Flood (Indoors)</p>\n<p>2:3:2 Zone</p>\n<p>3:2:2 Zone</p>\n<p>Arrowhead Zone</p><div class=\"cards\"><a class=\"card\" href=\"#person-defence\"><h3>Person Defence &amp; the Force</h3><p>Holding a force, protecting the danger zone and marking handlers.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#help-defence\"><h3>Help Defence &amp; Team Set-ups</h3><p>Marking as a pair, handler help, switches and set-ups against each stack.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#switching\"><h3>Switching &amp; Surrounding</h3><p>Switching on crossing cuts and surrounding stacks.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#house-zone\"><h3>House Zone (Indoors)</h3><p>2 chasers, 2 mids and a deep.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#clamp-flood\"><h3>Clamp &amp; Flood (Indoors)</h3><p>Indoor end zone defence.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#wall-zone\"><h3>Wall Zone (1-3-3) &amp; Zone Principles</h3><p>The junk/wall zone, containing a broken zone, switching to person D and zone variations.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#outdoor-zones\"><h3>Outdoor Zones</h3><p>2-3-2, 3-2-2, arrowhead and FSU.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#hybrid-defences\"><h3>Hybrid Defences: Fred &amp; Wilma</h3><p>Two zone/person hybrids built around a defender in the throwing lane.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#boxing-out\"><h3>Boxing Out</h3><p>Winning the 50/50 disc in the air.</p></a></div>"}, {"slug": "switching", "title": "Switching & Surrounding", "hero": "e7402d46b0b39d56eba9597fb8d4073d", "html": "<h2>Switching</h2>\n<p>Usually when we play match defence, everyone sticks with the same person the whole time as they run around the pitch. This works okay, but we can improve our efficiency and start working as a team by being aware of switching opportunities.</p>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"SrAYJpO0vPA\"></div>\n<p>Any time that offensive players cross paths could be an opportunity to switch. This is advantageous because defenders don't have to run as far and they can also maintain good positioning on the person they are marking.</p>\n<p>For example, in the first switch in the animation, defender 4 is able to stay under of the cutters, stopping easy short passes, while defender 3 can stay deep and be first to any hucks.</p>\n<p>Notice that at the moment the switch occurs, the attackers are between the two defenders. This is a very advantageous position for the defenders, and an extension of this positioning is known as surrounding.</p>\n\n<p>In the above animation Green is offence, Grey is Defence</p>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"ZdUbFaUZpsU\"></div>\n<h2>Surrounding</h2>\n<p>Many offences use structures like stacks, in which several offensive players cluster. Rather than continuing to match up, intelligent defences will take advantage by surrounding the cluster. When you are surrounding, there is no direction in which an attacker can go to get to the disc first.</p>\n"}, {"slug": "house-zone", "title": "House Zone (Indoors)", "hero": "fe05a477ccbce8eb687551652af79688", "html": "<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/b8b1d8bdb277a63da978729ceeca1fa6.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>Structure</p>\n<p>A house zone is named due to its structure (vaguely) resembling a house. There are 2 chasers, 2 mids, and a deep.</p>\n<p>With this zone, we force middle. The idea is that we force them to throw into the area that is clogged up by our mids. To do this, one chase should be forcing while the other stays close enough to the dump to stop swings.</p>\n<p>The mids should stay quite central and mark people closely when they cut into the central area.</p>\n<p>The deep is responsible for protecting against any deep shots.</p>\n<p>Usually if we are using this zone then we will collapse to a flood when they get close to our end zone.</p></div></div>\n"}, {"slug": "clamp-flood", "title": "Clamp & Flood (Indoors)", "hero": "23277dae77f16e04c313f77d7e402082", "html": "<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/602f10f9bed5fda45da895c75ebcc5cf.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>Clamp</p>\n<p>When we are playing indoors, overhead throws are much easier to use. A consequence of this is that we expect the force to be broken a lot more often, and we need to be especially careful near our end zone if we want to stop the other team from scoring.</p>\n<p>For this reason, even if there is only one player in our end zone, we usually want to set up a defender on each side of them so that both open side and break side throws are covered. This is called a clamp.</p>\n<p>When we are clamping one player, both defenders should stay close so that no easy shots open up through the middle. This situation also means that there is an extra offensive player upfield, so all defenders need to be ready to switch so that the most dangerous options are covered.</p>\n<p>When we are clamping two players, the defenders are not matched up 1-to-1. Instead, they should mark whichever cutter cuts in their direction and cover the open side of that cutter,</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/cff9e4d9991010a8ce5feea0e3793049.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>Flood</p>\n<p>A flood, sometimes known as a wall, is another end zone defence strategy used indoors. It involves 4 of our 5 players lining up at the front of our end zone, with one chase forcing the thrower towards the flood.</p>\n<p>Players in the flood should not just stand in position. They should mark players that come into their area and then pass them off to someone else when they move. This requires a lot of communication between players in the flood.</p>\n<p>The chase should always try to stop swings, meaning they should be right around to the side of the thrower. It also means that the force may change depending on where the disc is.</p></div></div>"}, {"slug": "outdoor-zones", "title": "Outdoor Zones", "hero": "d6056998739eb0cea2d27eea522a24a6", "html": "<nav class=\"toc\"><b>On this page</b><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"2:3:2 (Box Zone)\">2:3:2 (Box Zone)</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"3:2:2 (Clogging)\">3:2:2 (Clogging)</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Arrowhead\">Arrowhead</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"FSU\">FSU</button></nav>\n<h2>2:3:2 (Box Zone)</h2>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/c030ad0d638306ebffe741c2d9e52825.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>The 2-3-2 zone has two chasers, three mids (also known as the wall) and two deeps. Unlike arrowhead, when using this zone we are trying to prevent swings and force them towards the middle, where our wall is set up.</p>\n<p>Whichever chase is closest will set up a force towards the other chase. Their job is to stop swings so the force should be much further around than usual, not worrying about any throws up the pitch. The other chase should mark the open-side dump, it is their job to stop swings to the open side. The forcing changes when the disc is on the sideline, but we'll talk about that in a moment.</p>\n<p>The mids should all be working to mark out any cutters coming under. They may wish to actually face away from the disc most of the time so that they can see the cutters and get in front of them.</p>\n<p>The deeps should take half of the deep space each and mark out any potential deep options. They also need to communicate to the wall a lot so that they know what's coming if they are looking at the disc.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/b1e2cc866600a286253065015033d810.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>When the disc is on the sideline, we no longer want to force middle. Instead, we set up a sideline trap.</p>\n<p>The nearest chase puts on a hard no-around force, just like an arrowhead trap. The wall should then come in close to form the rest of a 4-person cup. Importantly, the second chase is not part of the cup. Instead they will set themselves up to prevent any big swings to the handler on the far side. The deeps still have a similar role but one may wish to come in closer to mark out throws over the wall.</p>\n<p>Delete section</p></div></div>\n<h2>3:2:2 (Clogging)</h2>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/4d6770499f1f56a41e4e9cbeb98f6c5b.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>This zone consists of 3 chasers (who effectively mark the 3 handlers), 2 mids (who pick up under cuts, and 2 deeps (who mark deep cutters).</p>\n<p>When the disc is in the centre, the middle chase puts on a flat force and the other 2 poach off so that they block the cutting lanes. This zone is designed to allow swings but stop any movement up the pitch.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/97833d0230b5832ef4eefb61e51b60b8.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>When the disc is on the sideline, we now force middle. Again, the other two chasers poach off into the lane. All of this once again stops any throws up the field and forces lots of swings, similar to how an arrowhead is sometimes used.</p>\n<p>The advantage this zone has over arrowhead is that it is less tiring for the front three, as the person who is forcing will switch and they don't all have to chase after the disc.</p>\n<p>Delete section</p></div></div></div>\n<h2>Arrowhead</h2>\n<h3>The Basics</h3>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/f8c0fc0430bb00f84af346c16ba698f5.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>The zone looks like this. As you can see, it makes the shape of an arrow, which is why we call it arrowhead. This zone is very useful in windy conditions, especially when the other team are going up-wind.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/4b99ace3b1b32454dd6d6c5146b6cee6.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>In a zone, players each have a position that they fill instead of a person that they mark. The positions are labelled in the diagram. The chase should always use a flat force on the disc.</p></div></div></div>\n<h3>Movement</h3>\n<p>The arrowhead moves around with the disc, with the chase and barbs always following it and the mid always staying behind them.</p>\n<h3>Sideline Trap</h3>\n<p>When the disc is on the sideline, it is a great opportunity to trap the opposition in. We do this by altering our positioning slightly, as shown below.</p>\n<div class=\"split\"><figure><img src=\"img/7805569c9abc486a49422cd0ea1abab2.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><div><p>The chase adjusts their positioning to go around the player on disc so that they are almost behind them. This stops them from dumping the disc back towards the centre. The barbs rotate around with one of them stopping throws horizontally across the pitch and the other blocking throws up the line. The mid should come in closer than usual to get between the barbs and stop throws into the middle of the pitch.</p></div></div>\n<h2>FSU</h2>\n<p>Some things to note about using FSU. You want to use it to disrupt a teams offence. If a team is coming out and putting in those offensive points over and over or you just want to make them think about something different, throw FSU. So it has the advantage of not just letting handlers work it up the pitch against a zone and the advantage of giving slightly less experienced throwers a hard time. By really tightly marking those dump cuts, you put a lot of pressure on weaker throwers and make them throw into poachy lanes. I wouldn't bring this out every point as people will realise how to work it soon enough but like other defences, it's a useful weapon in your arsenal.</p>\n<p>When to use!?</p>\n<p>If the other team is giving you problems with a strong horizontal 3/4 and when you throw an FSU.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"img/d607c1fc871ba712ee8231c9055dda73.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><p>FSU can largely be split into two parts, when the disc is in the middle and when the disc is on the sideline.</p>\n<p>3 man marks, 1 open side poach, 2 wing poaches and a deep poach. The 3 handler marks should be playing very tight man defence, really getting their bodies in the way and preventing easy resets. You want the offence to be disgruntled and hopefully with no reset and and some funny poaches upfield, they'll get into a high stall count situation.</p>\n<p>Personally, I think the open side poach has the toughest job. He needs to be aware of cuts coming both to the open side and to that very slight window cut between him and the force. I believe that he should make the window tight, facing across the pitch towards the open side. Facing this way gives the ability to see both cutters on the open side and the disc. Also it means that he can accelerate towards the bigger slice that needs covered.</p>\n<p>The wings need to be aware of anyone coming into those wide areas. As you can probably see, with only one deep, if the disc gets to a side, parallel or past the open lane poach then there is a lot of space upfield. Wings should drop off and not try to mark the person who gets the disc and resume their poach position.</p>\n<p>The deep does what the deep does. He needs to talk to the wings a lot if they have the opportunity to drop deeper to help out, all the better. There needs to be quite a lot of talking to the force about which throw to take out, especially with that window cut. In my opinion the wings should be shouting this while the deep talks to them and the open lane poach about incoming cuts. Whatever you're comfortable with.</p>\n<p>You can play a similar shape against vertical stacks if you like. Also, I think some teams flat force the disc, have two lane poaches and two deep of the play, especially if it's not that windy.</p>\n<p>On the sideline, I think there's a bit more variation because you tend to drop the handler marks and it becomes a bit more zoneish, with a trap on the sideline. The second part of the image is how I would play it. Theoretically you could keep the same shape as in the middle though, but move your lane poach respectively. If you have a sideline trap you are familiar with already and you can see how the players can switch from FSU in the middle to the sideline trap then use that. In my diagram I would move the handler marks to guard those handler cuts, right wing moves into the lane, open lane poach takes the IO lane etc.</p>\n<p>SEE ANIMATION BELOW:</p>\n\n<p>What if the offence decides to only use two handlers? or what if they open to using four handlers 3 cutters!?</p>\n<p>The first thing to consider is that FSU is more of a reactionary strategy to certain styles of play.</p>\n<p>However, if you tried to set it up against a 2 handler setup, and poached that third handler mark position, a lot of the time teams will be quick to bring a handler back because it looks very zoneish. If they don't then probably you want that third handler mark to act as a break side poach or as another deep but to be honest I'm not really sure. Obviously they'll figure out fast enough, but again, the point of FSU is to catch an offence a little off guard.</p>\n<p>Against 4, depends a little on the setup of the 4. For example, if your 4 are 2 in the middle and 2 out wide in a bucket shape then the two in the middle can be man marked while the 2 out wide fall quite naturally into the zone of the wings. This actually ends up giving the FSU an extra man who used to mark the third handler who can mess things up wherever he goes. Having said this, I'd still prefer to play this set against FSU because it gives the defence more to think about regarding their poaches and flips the tables a bit.</p>"}, {"slug": "boxing-out", "title": "Boxing Out", "hero": "d6056998739eb0cea2d27eea522a24a6", "html": "<p>Imagine:</p>\n<p>Your favorite thrower gets the disc and you immediately streak deep down the field. You look back to the thrower and as expected, the huck is thrown. The disc goes high, but it’s floating, hanging, and you’re not going to be able to catch the disc perfectly in stride. Your defender has a chance to catch up and make a play on the disc. So is this purely a jump ball — whoever jumps the highest wins? Or is there some way to turn a 50/50 disc into a 100% disc for you?</p>\n<p>As a predominantly short team its quite tedious to constantly get roofed on imperfect hucks. So we want to find a way to turn the odds more in our favor. There are three key point to consider in order to improve in the air.</p>\n<p>Read the disc.</p>\n<p>Gain and maintain body position - keep yourself between the disc and the defender (Boxing Out)</p>\n<p>Go up with your outside hand</p>\n<p>Here are a few videos from some Americans to give some tips :D</p>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"ELkabwCTT24\"></div>\n<div class=\"video\" data-yt=\"nWkhs2VfA0I\"></div>"}, {"slug": "training", "title": "Training", "hero": "3ee335dffe7d0f1f1c35b1fb6b4365be", "html": "<p class=\"lede\">Everything for planning and running sessions. Fitness plans now have their own <a href=\"#fitness\">Fitness section</a>.</p><div class=\"cards\"><a class=\"card\" href=\"#session-plan\"><h3>How a Session Runs</h3><p>How a typical session is structured.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#warmup\"><h3>Warm-up</h3><p>FrisFA warm-up, mobility and S&amp;C options.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#training-plan\"><h3>2025/26 Training Plan</h3><p>The 2025/26 offence and defence topics, fortnight by fortnight.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#drills\"><h3>Drill Library</h3><p>Every drill in one place, filterable by players, category and skill.</p></a></div><h2>Drills by focus</h2><div class=\"cards\"><a class=\"card\" href=\"#drills-intro\"><h3>Fundamentals</h3><p>100 throws, Super 70s, pivot and break-force drills.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#drills-throwing\"><h3>Throwing</h3><p>Box, Swiss, lead passes, breaks and hucking.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#drills-endzone\"><h3>Endzone</h3><p>Mushroom and Seattle.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#drills-defence\"><h3>Defence</h3><p>D circle, positioning, choosing a shoulder.</p></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"#drills-agility\"><h3>Agility</h3><p>Hockey stops, slaloms and the footwork gauntlet.</p></a></div>"}, {"slug": "session-plan", "title": "How a Session Runs", "hero": "6402936932dc6ed8b68cd656a43c40c0", "html": "<p>Here, we aim to consolidate essential information for training. We start with an effective and efficient warm-up routine to prevent injuries both at tournaments and during training sessions. Following this, we provide a comprehensive breakdown of the training plan for the pre-season and Terms 1, 2, and 3, covering both indoor and outdoor ultimate frisbee. This consolidation is designed to reduce the workload involved in training planning and to offer a clear overview of this week's training schedule.</p>\n<p>As a general rule trainings will follow the structure below:</p>\n<p>Typical warm up which is the GB one (found here) that the players know. Then go into more game realistic part of a warm up with throwing to the cutting partners that we run. Would also have movement up and down the pitch with no defence just working on timings of cuts and again running the different patterns etc</p>\n<p>I'd then isolate particular things that I want to work on which are either weaknesses or strengths and focusing on how to improve those. Try to keep everything realistic and relate it to something from games or at least a scenario- disc coming in from the sideline type thing.</p>\n<p>Then a game based around that area- for above example any turnover gets taken to the side-line. Something like 10 pull so the same team starts on offence/defence each time.</p>\n<p>Then I'd focus on either something that's come up within that game or another part again. Have a game scenario again to work on it.</p>\n<p>Then big game but award double points for certain things. For example: use of a side line play gets a point type thing.</p>"}, {"slug": "warmup", "title": "Warm-up", "hero": "3ee335dffe7d0f1f1c35b1fb6b4365be", "html": "<p>Within this segment we give you several different options for warmups</p>\n<h3>Option 1:</h3>\n<figure><img src=\"img/b5a5bb28a19143695153046394617282.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h3>Option 2:</h3>\n<p>FrisFA Warm-Up - 2x6 Cones @ 5m intervals</p>\n<ul><li>Neck Tilts, Twists &amp; Rolls &amp; Shoulder Rolls</li><li>Splits Arms Up/Down + Alt. Open Arms Wide + Shoulder Pats</li><li>Arm Circles - Wide Stance with Alt. Side Angle Reaches Overhead &amp; Lunge</li><li>Wide Stance Torso &amp; Arm Swings = Throw Backhands Behind - Hip Rotations</li><li>Reach Up on Toes + Wrist Rotations, Fold Forwards Pedalling &amp; Torso Swings,</li></ul>\n<p>D-Dog &amp; Pedalling, Up-Dog + Torso Twists, D-Dog Pedal, Walk Hands back in</p>\n<ul><li>Deep Squat + Side/Up Rotations, Fold Forwards + Pedalling &amp; Torso Swing</li><li>Reach Up on Toes, Squats Down &amp; Touch Hands to ground (Marking Wide)</li><li>Wide Stance, Big Ball Rotations (both directions)</li><li>‘Feed the Chickens’ Low Hamstring Sweeps with Foot Pivots (inside &amp; outside)</li><li>1 Leg Balance &amp; Ankle Rotations + Partner Pushing Off Balance</li><li>Alt. Quad Stretch + 1 Leg SuperHeroes Extension + Knee-Chest Pull</li><li>Jog Out, 50% Run Back</li><li>2x Do-Si-Do (Left lane always Behind) or 1½ Circles around Cones</li></ul>\n<p>Mobility</p>\n<ul><li>Reach Up on Toes + Wrists, Fold Forwards Pedalling &amp; Torso Side-Side Swing,</li></ul>\n<p>Down-Dog &amp; Pedalling, Deep Low Lunge + Rocking/Splits + Inside Arm Twist Up</p>\n<ul><li>Alt. Pull Knee-Chest on Toes + Lunge Walk + Twist &amp; 1 Arm BH Throw Behind</li><li>Frankenstein’s Kicks - Figure 4 Alt. Glute Stretches + Wide Foot-Block</li><li>Side-Shuffle, Open-Gates (from behind), Alt. Sides @ Cone, Then Close-Gates</li><li>Alt. Side-Shuffle + Low Mark + Foot Block Kick + 180° Squat Jump @ Cones</li><li>Alt. Open-Gate-Sumo Side-Lunge (Both Sides) + 2 Forehand Throw Motions</li><li>A-Skips - B-Skips (Pull Leg Back) - C-Skips (A-Skip + Same Knee Side Raise)</li><li>High Knees (Every 3rd, then normal) -&gt; Sprinting</li><li>Bouncing on Toes (Fwds/Bkwds/Side-Side) -&gt; Crossovers Sideways Running</li><li>Carioca Twists (both sides)</li><li>Heel-Bum (Knees Remain in Front!) -&gt; Sprinting</li><li>Lying Prone (Face Down) Alt. Leg Swings (Crossing Behind) Shoulders Flat</li></ul>\n<p>Strength &amp; Conditioning</p>\n<ul><li>‘Superheroes’ High Plank + Alt. Opp. Arm &amp; Leg Raises (+1 Elbow-Knee Touch)</li><li>High Side Plank with Top Leg Raises &amp;/or Arm Reaches Under Thru &amp; Back Up</li><li>Low Plank + Alt. Bent Leg Glute Pulses Up + Straight Leg Raises &amp; Tap Down</li><li>Single-Leg Glute Bridges (Alt. 1 Leg Straight Up) Walk-Out &amp; Back In</li><li>(in pairs) Nordic Hamstring Curls or Solo Kneeling Leaning Back &amp; Forth</li><li>Single-Leg Pistol Squats (assisted)</li><li>‘Ladder’ 3 Side Steps and Lateral Jump (Hold Alt. Knee Up in Skater Position)</li><li>Lateral Lunges (Side Push Offs)</li><li>Skater Lateral Jumps (continuous)</li><li>3 Step Alt. Skipping High Jumps &amp; Reach Up (with Alt. Knee Drive Up)</li><li>Bounding</li><li>Quick Broad Jumps Fwds/Bkwds, Side-Side, Diagonally Fwds/Bkwds</li><li>Burpees</li><li>2xCompete for High Disc (+Shoulder Bumps)</li><li>2 Cones Forward then Fast Feet, Hard Stop</li><li>2 Cones Forward, 1 Cone Backpedalling</li><li>70% Sprint out &amp; Jog back</li><li>Zig-Zags = Fast Diagonal Cuts around Cones . (Hard Low Plant &amp; Turn)</li><li>Mountain Climbers + 80% Sprint</li><li>Crab +Alt. Straight Leg Up &amp; Hand-Toe +TDip</li><li>Down-Dog Tricep Push-Ups + 90% Sprint</li><li>30m L Side-Steps, 30m R, 30m 100% Sprint</li><li>Resistance Band Pulls - Tricep Push-Ups</li><li>Med Ball Toss - Alt. Lunge &amp; K-Bell Side Lifts</li><li>Single Leg Romanian Dead-Lifts</li><li>Resistance Sprinting (10x80m) - Lunge Walk - Horse Stance Low Squat Hold</li></ul>\n<p>Further work includes running the cutting patterns for practice to instill knowledge:</p>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/3bcd6b5e87e6869996cfe5b4e3f39d29.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/ce49d5674b69c2eadf61660ec39631c4.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"><p>Vert Stack Cutting Patterns, Clear to the sidelines</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><img src=\"img/f6e98d5c12baf3f27a94f2397df147f4.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><div class=\"body\"></div></div></div>\n<figure><img src=\"img/12c8fdda5db40b42454cb47c89c1a2a8.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>"}, {"slug": "training-plan", "title": "2025/26 Training Plan", "hero": "3ee335dffe7d0f1f1c35b1fb6b4365be", "html": "<p class=\"lede\">Each fortnight pairs an offensive and a defensive theme. Click a topic to jump to the page or drill that covers it.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"plan\"><thead><tr><th>Weeks</th><th>Offence</th><th>Defence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=\"plan-break\"><td colspan=\"3\">Term 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1–2</strong></td><td><a href=\"#vert-stack\">Vert Stack</a></td><td><a href=\"#drill-buffering\">Match (tight)</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>3–4</strong></td><td><a href=\"#iso\">ISO (isolation)</a></td><td><a href=\"#drill-triangle-forcing\">Force sideline + middle</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>5–6</strong></td><td><a href=\"#endzone-outdoors\">Endzone structures</a></td><td><a href=\"#clamp-flood\">Clamp</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>7–8</strong></td><td><a href=\"#drill-beating-poaches-side-stack\">Side stack</a></td><td><a href=\"#switching\">Switching + surrounding</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>9–10</strong></td><td><a href=\"#drill-break-side-reset\">Reset systems</a></td><td><a href=\"#drill-bracket-back-of-stack\">Bracketing</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>11–12</strong></td><td><a href=\"#drill-fast-break-back-line\">Fast break / transition</a></td><td><a href=\"#house-zone\">House zone</a></td></tr><tr class=\"plan-break\"><td colspan=\"3\">Christmas holidays</td></tr><tr class=\"plan-break\"><td colspan=\"3\">Term 2</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1–2</strong></td><td><a href=\"#drill-three-player-weave\">Dominators (3-person weave)</a></td><td>Flashing the lane</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3–4</strong></td><td><a href=\"#drill-handler-weave\">Handler weave (give-and-go)</a></td><td><a href=\"#switching\">Pre-switch / cross-match</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>5–6</strong></td><td><a href=\"#drill-reverse-zipper\">Pull plays / set plays</a></td><td><a href=\"#drill-junk-zone-roles\">Junk / wall defences</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>7–8</strong></td><td><a href=\"#drill-flood-side-stack\">Flood offence</a></td><td>Hex defence</td></tr><tr><td><strong>9–10</strong></td><td><a href=\"#drill-hex-progression\">Hex offence</a></td><td>Hex defence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><a class=\"btn-link\" href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1W8Az7METsZ6-EjVtjKTX1h6fy2Nt3BN850h8P6r7rz0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open the live Google Sheet ↗</a></p><p class=\"muted\">If the sheet changes, the table here needs updating to match.</p>"}, {"slug": "drills", "title": "Drill Library", "hero": "3ee335dffe7d0f1f1c35b1fb6b4365be", "html": ""}, {"slug": "fitness-26-weeks", "title": "26 Weeks of Hell", "hero": "3ee335dffe7d0f1f1c35b1fb6b4365be", "html": "<p class=\"lede\">The long-form plan from the GB U23 workout guide: weekly tables below, with tutorial videos for every exercise at the bottom.</p><nav class=\"toc\"><b>On this page</b><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Overview of the fitness plan from week 1 to week 25\">Overview of the fitness plan from week 1 to week 25</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Week 1 - 5\">Week 1 - 5</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Week 6 - 9\">Week 6 - 9</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Week 10 - 13\">Week 10 - 13</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Week 14 - 16\">Week 14 - 16</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Week 17 - 19\">Week 17 - 19</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Week 20\">Week 20</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Week 21\">Week 21</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Week 22\">Week 22</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Week 23\">Week 23</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Week 24\">Week 24</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Week 25\">Week 25</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Week 26\">Week 26</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Endurance\">Endurance</button><button type=\"button\" data-goto=\"Tutorial Videos\">Tutorial Videos</button></nav>\n<h2>Overview of the fitness plan from week 1 to week 25</h2>\n<figure><img src=\"img/ab17725ed28afa9cec46363e209d248e.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p>The warmup should be the same as pre match. Please do to avoid injury and ensure maximum effectiveness of fitness and training.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"img/347eff9035faae41b9744b4958781942.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Week 1 - 5</h2>\n<figure><img src=\"img/94472cf1524cdc8724b90792b81eabce.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Week 6 - 9</h2>\n<figure><img src=\"img/ceefce56fa1016d6e245f0d6e89f14f4.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Week 10 - 13</h2>\n<figure><img src=\"img/2e71ddadd6573b6da3edc666a320cbad.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Week 14 - 16</h2>\n<figure><img src=\"img/e63e732c4729e6874fd403eb59a1dd97.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Week 17 - 19</h2>\n<figure><img src=\"img/82e5a06497bdf887e6860bf5c31d3cea.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<figure><img src=\"img/7f109a791a360b311b82debefb9f58db.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<figure><img src=\"img/81ad9757a83cc63d6ed146c169e9e5ed.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Week 20</h2>\n<figure><img src=\"img/e057c808fc236e3d5bfd7a88523a0e64.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Week 21</h2>\n<figure><img src=\"img/9624663f75a9d2c913ccef56c6a1a488.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Week 22</h2>\n<figure><img src=\"img/e4725d6cbcfa03bcef2522003dea7adc.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Week 23</h2>\n<figure><img src=\"img/c7a16d14f044fbc7006db2407b80d380.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Week 24</h2>\n<figure><img src=\"img/6755756185bb9639ba83e67e81f6f52d.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Week 25</h2>\n<figure><img src=\"img/d4e595384908799b2424e15bf24ef47d.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Week 26</h2>\n<figure><img src=\"img/d3383b3917c0168c717d45e5f12150bc.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Endurance</h2>\n<figure><img src=\"img/18848fa316b03f9fce94f7bb11ef2aa6.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<h2>Tutorial Videos</h2>\n<ul><ul class=\"vids\"><li>Strength:</li><li>Lunge Matrix: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ESYkudkZsQ8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ESYkudkZsQ8</a></li><li>Press ups: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G3K1CRO46rc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G3K1CRO46rc</a></li><li>Reverse Plank: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1s1bPYBPERU\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1s1bPYBPERU</a></li><li>Single Arm Band Pull: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c0Slq2ucwHg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c0Slq2ucwHg</a></li><li>Single Leg Calf Raises: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IgYD8WraLmw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IgYD8WraLmw</a></li><li>Single Leg Squats: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X_qe9omeE3E\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X_qe9omeE3E</a></li><li>Resistance Band High Pull: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OuHJevUlEHw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OuHJevUlEHw</a></li><li>Overhead Single Arm Band Press: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8Mxrz-S6XD0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8Mxrz-S6XD0</a></li><li>Band Single Leg Hip Extensions: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q5F_tYiR2fQ&amp;list=PL28D4CD8F0B91DB9E\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q5F_tYiR2fQ&amp;list=PL28D4CD8F0B91DB9E</a></li><li>Pullups/Single Arm Band Pull Down: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NZEQBYS9iKc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NZEQBYS9iKc</a></li><li>Single Leg GLUT. Bridge: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vdmlNaXSjd4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vdmlNaXSjd4</a></li><li>Slide To Hollow: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RuEHVk-4-6A\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RuEHVk-4-6A</a></li><li>Seated Band Pulls: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xz29bq7gVbg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xz29bq7gVbg</a></li><li>Nordic Curls: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gB63fScCb5k\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gB63fScCb5k</a></li><li>Core:</li><li>Hollow Hold: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BQCdzRPE9Ao\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BQCdzRPE9Ao</a></li><li>Side Plank: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aFIeCPokJFE\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aFIeCPokJFE</a></li><li>Band Walk: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zcqc6VRRpew\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zcqc6VRRpew</a></li><li>Band Hip Adduction: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HQqRq4hVP9w\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HQqRq4hVP9w</a></li><li>YWTA: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3coyboUC6g0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3coyboUC6g0</a></li><li>Single Leg Hip Thrust: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VBwPtkNCnOc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VBwPtkNCnOc</a></li><li>Side Plank Adductor Hold: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MRJI7pdUeuw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MRJI7pdUeuw</a></li><li>Bicycle Crunch: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nbubf5wCsKg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nbubf5wCsKg</a></li><li>Front Plank Picking Up Leg: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Uan-Wvyc5i0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Uan-Wvyc5i0</a></li><li>Deadbugs: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_twlz5HmEU\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_twlz5HmEU</a></li><li>Reverse Trunk Twist: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UzfVaDFIJoY\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UzfVaDFIJoY</a></li><li>Plyometrics and Sprints:</li><li>Sit to Jump: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/Eumo7R8VX8g\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/shorts/Eumo7R8VX8g</a></li><li>Kneeling to Jump: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=W8XwSNt8P5A\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=W8XwSNt8P5A</a></li><li>Ankle Overs: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=spDw2OWtArU\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=spDw2OWtArU</a></li><li>Explosive Knee Drives: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LIO49dCk858\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LIO49dCk858</a></li><li>CMJ (Counter Movement Jump): <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MshBlueDsDk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MshBlueDsDk</a></li><li>Forward Jumps: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ONHu0TGcvYM\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ONHu0TGcvYM</a></li><li>Lateral hops: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TGHWuREdK3I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TGHWuREdK3I</a></li><li>Mountain Climbers: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DyeZM-_VnRc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DyeZM-_VnRc</a></li><li>Speed Skaters: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EkESodXYDRM\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EkESodXYDRM</a></li><li>Single Leg Hops: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Szvf3frYgJE\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Szvf3frYgJE</a></li><li>Box Squats: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WzhbFsNaO7c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WzhbFsNaO7c</a></li></ul></ul>"}, {"slug": "cutting-basics", "title": "Cutting Basics", "hero": null, "html": "\n<p class=\"lede\">How to cut so you get free and are easy to throw to. Everything else in our offence builds on this.</p>\n\n<h2>The throwing lane</h2>\n<p>The throwing lane is the strip of field straight upfield of the thrower on the open side, away from the mark. It's the easiest throw on the pitch, so the whole team's job is to keep it clear for whoever is cutting into it.</p>\n\n<h2>Going deep</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Commit.</strong> Cut hard in one direction and link cuts with sharp turns. Changing direction every few steps confuses your thrower and gives the defender time to recover.</li>\n<li><strong>Stay wide of the lane.</strong> You don't block it for others, the throw doesn't have to go round you, and you can watch the disc over your shoulder instead of chasing one over your head.</li>\n<li><strong>Start shallow.</strong> Begin just upfield of the disc, so you're at full speed when the throw arrives out in front of you. If you're already deep and waiting, it becomes a 50/50 with your defender.</li>\n<li><strong>Know the point of no return.</strong> Past a certain depth your defender stops following you, because they know they'd catch the disc up. If you get there and the disc hasn't gone, turn and come back under.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"cut-deep\"></div>\n\n<h2>Coming under</h2>\n<p>Cut in at an angle to the lane, not straight at the thrower. It helps three ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Bigger target.</strong> Running across the line of the throw makes it easy to adjust to a disc that's slightly off.</li>\n<li><strong>You beat your defender.</strong> To cut in at an angle you have to run past them, so they end up behind you with no chance of the block.</li>\n<li><strong>You catch facing upfield.</strong> You can see the next cutter and throw the continuation before the mark arrives.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you don't have an angle, go deep first to make one. Choose the under cut when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>your defender is sitting deep;</li>\n<li>you're past the point of no return;</li>\n<li>a teammate is already going deep, so the space underneath is free;</li>\n<li>the thrower can't make the long throw (they'll show you with a fake or by turning away).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"cut-under\"></div>\n\n<h2>Talking to your thrower</h2>\n<p>Thrower and cutter communicate in three ways: talking (we don't do it enough), making cuts so committed the thrower can read them, and the thrower faking to say \"not that one, go again\". The thrower owns the decision: they pick the throw to take on, not the loudest cutter.</p>\n\n<h2>Continuation</h2>\n<p>Continuation (or flow) is a run of quick passes in the same direction before the defence can reset. The next cutter reads the play and starts early, so they're already sprinting into space as the catch is made. Give the new thrower a shout as they catch.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Too early:</strong> you're standing in the space, waiting, and your defender catches up.</li>\n<li><strong>Too late:</strong> the thrower has to wait and the mark gets set.</li>\n<li><strong>Right:</strong> the thrower releases straight away and you run onto the disc at full speed.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"cut-continuation\"></div>\n<p>Practise it with <a href=\"#drill-shadow-continuation\">Shadow Continuation</a>, <a href=\"#drill-all-in-one-receiving\">the All-in-One Receiving Square</a> and <a href=\"#drill-attack-the-under\">Defensive Run / Attack the Disc</a>.</p>\n"}, {"slug": "catching", "title": "Catching", "hero": null, "html": "\n<p class=\"lede\">Every completed pass needs a catch, so catching deserves as much practice as throwing. It's also quicker to learn.</p>\n\n<h2>The clap catch (pancake)</h2>\n<p>Your default. Two hands on the disc gives you the best chance of holding it, and clapping down onto it leaves room for error when the wind moves it up or down.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use your legs</strong> to get your chest behind the disc: step across, sink or jump. Your eyes are then in line with it, and a fumble bounces off your chest for a second go.</li>\n<li><strong>Draw your arms in</strong> towards your chest as you catch to soften hard throws.</li>\n<li><strong>Watch it all the way in.</strong> Try to read the writing on the disc. A common turnover is thinking about the next throw before the catch is made.</li>\n<li><strong>Which hand on top?</strong> Practise both. For a flat disc into your chest, either works: pick the one that sets up your next throw. For a tilted disc, use whichever hand stops you twisting your arms.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>The two-handed rim catch (claw)</h2>\n<p>Grip the rim with both hands when you're at full stretch, under pressure from a defender, or sprinting (it's awkward to run with your hands in clap position). It lets you take the disc earlier. Also use it for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low discs:</strong> easier to get fingers under than an arm; thumbs on top usually feels natural.</li>\n<li><strong>Laying out:</strong> you can let go with one hand to help your landing, instead of landing on your shoulders with both hands clamped on.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>One-handed catches</h2>\n<p>When there's no time for two hands, or you need maximum reach (high, or far to the side). Catch the rim and clamp down to stop the spin. Whether your thumb or fingers go underneath depends on whether you're taking the leading edge or snagging the trailing edge as it passes.</p>\n\n<h2>Receiving on under cuts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Catch and move straight into a throw.</strong> Even a tiny fake. It gives you the chance of an instant pass, or shifts the mark as it arrives. Make it a habit in drills and games.</li>\n<li><strong>Scan as you cut.</strong> Use your peripheral vision and listen for teammates calling for the continuation, but make the catch first.</li>\n<li><strong>Feel your defender.</strong> Where they are tells you which way to turn: if they're overcommitting to the open side, the break throw is there straight away.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Receiving deep</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Read the disc:</strong> height, speed, tilt and wind. If you can see the underside of a disc tilted steeply towards you, it's probably going over your head. Turn and sprint to the spot rather than backpedalling, then find the disc again.</li>\n<li><strong>Win position.</strong> If you'd lose a straight race, step between your opponent and where the disc will come down. Hold them off (box out) as it passes over, then run onto it. See <a href=\"#boxing-out\">Boxing Out</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Commit.</strong> Decide whether you need to go at your highest point or can take it as it drops. Don't get drawn into jumping early: know where you can catch it and fight for that spot.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Practise with <a href=\"#drill-catching-box\">the Catching Box</a>, <a href=\"#drill-reading-the-disc\">Reading the Disc</a> and <a href=\"#drill-aerial-battles\">Aerial Battles</a>.</p>\n"}, {"slug": "side-stack", "title": "Side Stack", "hero": null, "html": "\n<p class=\"lede\">A simple structure that uses everything in <a href=\"#cutting-basics\">Cutting Basics</a>: put the stack on a sideline and give one cutter the whole space in front of the disc.</p>\n\n<h2>Set-up</h2>\n<p>Two handlers on the disc, everyone else in a vertical stack along one sideline. The throwing lane is clear and there's always space in front of the disc. It works because players attack space one at a time, then get out of the way.</p>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"ss-setup\"></div>\n\n<h2>Primary cuts</h2>\n<p>The first cutter can come from anywhere in the stack. They can go deep straight away (staying wide of the lane) or set up an angled under cut, and keep cutting in the space until they get the disc or reach the clearing spot. Then they return to the front of the stack, clearing the space for the next cutter.</p>\n\n<h2>Continuation cuts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The continuation cut <strong>always comes from the back of the stack.</strong></li>\n<li>It starts as the first cutter makes their first turn, using the space near the stack to lose a defender and find an angle.</li>\n<li>Done well, the thrower gets two options in sequence, e.g. an under followed by a deep strike.</li>\n<li><strong>No more than two players cut at once.</strong> Delay your cut until the previous cutter is heading for the clearing spot.</li>\n<li>Expect the previous cutter to get the disc, and adjust your cut when they do.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"ss-continuation\"></div>\n\n<h2>After a catch</h2>\n<p>New cutters keep cycling out of the stack while the handlers reorganise: one becomes the reset, one fills the front of the stack, and the old front-of-stack player joins the queue. The receiver is now a handler until they push back into the stack.</p>\n\n<h2>Resets and swings</h2>\n<p>If nobody gets it, turn infield and engage the reset. Either keep the disc on the same side (up the line, a \"plinko\" cut round the back, or a handler give-and-go) and carry on cutting, or swing it to the far side. On a swing, the stack floods across to the other sideline and only the next cutter stays to set up a new cut. Swings also happen by accident (a throw to a poached player, a cross-field huck), so watch for them and flood.</p>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"ss-swing\"></div>\n\n<h2>Six-cutter side stack</h2>\n<p>Some teams play one handler and six stacked cutters. First cuts come from the back (C6) or up the line from the front (C1), and cutters clear into the middle of the stack. A system sometimes called \"Storm\" cycles these sequences all point: unders from the back clear to the front, up-line cuts from the front clear to the back, and when the disc changes side the stack rebuilds on the new sideline. Near the end zone, switch to a central stack to finish (see <a href=\"#vert-stack\">Vert Stack</a>).</p>\n\n<h2>Your job as a cutter, every second</h2>\n<div class=\"tipbox\"><strong>Attacking space</strong> (cutting for the disc), <strong>preserving space</strong> (clearing hard out of the lane) or <strong>creating space</strong> (joining the stack and flooding on a swing). If you're doing none of these, you're in the way.</div>\n\n<h2>Strengths and weaknesses</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Good:</strong> clean one-on-ones with nobody else in the lane, lots of space, and easy to learn because it runs on principles, not set plays. Everyone handles and cuts, which is great for development.</li>\n<li><strong>Watch out for:</strong> a defence that poaches the isolated cutter, or a deep guard who makes the first cut from the far side very hard. Spreading out (e.g. a <a href=\"#ho-stack\">horizontal stack</a>) or starting the first cutter in the lane can help.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Practise with <a href=\"#drill-side-stack-flow\">Side Stack Flow</a> and <a href=\"#drill-beating-poaches-side-stack\">Beating Poaches in Side Stack</a>.</p>\n"}, {"slug": "person-defence", "title": "Person Defence & the Force", "hero": null, "html": "\n<p class=\"lede\">Person defence is a team effort: a strong mark takes away the break side, and everyone upfield protects the open side. We get blocks together by taking away options and forcing hard throws late in the count.</p>\n\n<h2>The mark</h2>\n<p>The mark is the most important defender on the pitch: at no other time do you control so much of the field. <strong>Hold your force.</strong> Not getting broken matters far more than a hand block, because every break throw undoes the work of everyone upfield.</p>\n<h3>Body position</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Hips low and back, feet shoulder-width apart and slightly staggered.</li>\n<li>Arms wide and hands low: many break throws go under the arms.</li>\n<li>Head up, moving with small, quick steps.</li>\n<li>Not too far (no pressure), not too close (contact or disc-space fouls), and don't lean: a leaning mark is easy to get round.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Approaching</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>On the way in, first get between the thrower and their most dangerous throw (usually the break), then close in. Don't overrun them.</li>\n<li>Glance over your shoulder at upfield threats.</li>\n<li>Make yourself big on the break side.</li>\n<li>Start the count once you're within 3 m.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Through the count</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"plan\"><thead><tr><th>Stall</th><th>Priority</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>0</strong></td><td>Shut the break side and the immediate continuation; start counting from 3 m.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>1–2</strong></td><td>Close in, get big, cut down angles, ignore fakes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>3–6</strong></td><td>Aggressive, active marking with low hands and short steps.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>7</strong></td><td>Shout \"SEVEN!\" so the team knows they only have three seconds left to shut their player down.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>7–9</strong></td><td>Ease back slightly, hold the force, don't bite, don't foul.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>10</strong></td><td>Hold your position. A hurried throw under pressure is your best chance of a block. Definitely don't foul.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Priorities on the mark</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stop the shown throw.</strong> If they're set for a backhand, pressure the backhand while still holding the force.</li>\n<li><strong>Pressure the huck.</strong> A big wind-up is your cue to get across.</li>\n<li><strong>Make them pivot.</strong> It kills the early throws, eats the count and puts them in awkward positions.</li>\n<li><strong>Read hips, shoulders and knees,</strong> not the head, eyes or waving disc.</li>\n<li><strong>Listen to the sideline.</strong> A teammate behind the thrower calls \"left\" or \"right\" to move you (only as far as you can still hold the force). \"FRONT\" means abandon the force and stop the upfield throw: only for an emergency or a sure turnover at stall 9.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Upfield: protect space, not the person</h2>\n<p>Marking is a race where only the attacker knows where the finish line is. If you follow every move you'll lose. Instead, decide which races you must win:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Open-side unders, the danger zone.</strong> Right in front of the thrower, no help from the mark or teammates, the easiest throw. Never get beaten here.</li>\n<li><strong>Deep cuts.</strong> Little help from the mark, but the disc hangs in the air and teammates can help.</li>\n<li><strong>Break-side cuts.</strong> The mark protects these.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"pd-zones\"></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Keep a buffer.</strong> Stand a couple of steps off, between your player and the danger zone, feet and hips facing the space you protect. Accelerate when they do.</li>\n<li><strong>Stretch the buffer</strong> when your player is far from the disc (past the point of no return) or when you have help deep. That's your chance to help others.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep them in front of you.</strong> Don't let them get round your back and out of sight. Be an obstacle (legally), and in the danger zone get between them and the disc.</li>\n<li><strong>Every time the disc moves, the danger zone moves.</strong> Call \"UP!\" on every throw, check the disc, and re-set. A loud, long \"UP!\" means a floating disc: forget your player and get bodies under it.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"pd-buffer\"></div>\n<p>These priorities can change with the opponent or field position. Against a great deep receiver who isn't a strong thrower, give them the unders. Near their own end zone a metre gain matters much less than a 30 m lead pass, so you might give up short gains to stop the deep.</p>\n\n<h2>Marking handlers</h2>\n<p>Handlers work in a small space, often start from still, and want a reset plus a continuation rather than yards. Protect the part of the field the mark isn't covering:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Handler on the break side:</strong> deny the up-line cut, but stay close enough to stop an easy swing.</li>\n<li><strong>Handler on the open side, disc in the middle:</strong> protect the upfield throw and pressure the throw round the back.</li>\n<li><strong>Handler on the open side, disc on the sideline:</strong> the mark takes the up-line, so get really tight to stop the swing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Shut down their first cut: making a handler cut twice puts the offence under pressure at a high count. If they do get it, stop the immediate continuation first, then set your force. And re-set the moment your player throws: that's when they're most dangerous (give-and-go).</p>\n\n<h2>Defensive mindset</h2>\n<p>D-line is a mental battle: you'll often concede more than you score, and much of the work goes unnoticed. Play the percentages, make a plan for each point, and take the little wins: shutting the open side, not getting broken, pressuring a huck, helping a teammate deep.</p>\n<p>Practise with <a href=\"#drill-danger-zone-boxes\">Danger Zone Boxes</a>, <a href=\"#drill-open-side-unders\">Open-Side Unders</a>, <a href=\"#drill-protecting-the-upline\">Protecting the Up-line Cut</a> and <a href=\"#drill-triangle-marking\">Triangle Marking</a>.</p>\n"}, {"slug": "help-defence", "title": "Help Defence & Team Set-ups", "hero": null, "html": "\n<p class=\"lede\">Once everyone can hold their own player, the next step is defending as a unit: doubling up for a few seconds, helping in the lanes and setting up as a team at stoppages. This is still person defence, not a zone.</p>\n\n<h2>Defence as a process</h2>\n<p>Two aims: <strong>contain</strong> (keep the disc far from the end zone, make them throw lots of passes, stop continuations) and <strong>pressure</strong> (on the sideline, late in the count, tight on your player). Help defence is mostly about containing, with the odd block from diving into a space while your own player isn't a threat. Help, then <strong>recover</strong> or <strong>switch</strong>.</p>\n\n<h2>Marking as a pair</h2>\n<p>When two attackers are close together, two defenders can \"sandwich\" them: one under, one deep. You share responsibility for both. The under defender takes the first under cut and the deep defender gets tight on the other player.</p>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"hd-sandwich\"></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Only do it if they're close enough for the under defender to cover both unders.</li>\n<li>If both cut the same way, take the bigger threat and give your partner time.</li>\n<li>In play, cutters often pass close to each other. Spot it early and double up for a few seconds.</li>\n<li>Switch to stop a real threat, not to save yourself a run. Call it loudly.</li>\n<li>Example: your player cuts in, then clears to the break side with their back turned. Instead of following, wait on the open side a moment to see whether there's a free cutter to pick up; if not, recover.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Handler defenders helping in the lanes</h2>\n<p>Offences rarely throw backwards or sideways early in the count, so a handler's defender can drop into a cutting lane for the first few seconds to take away upfield throws, then recover and get tight late in the count. Or stay in the lane and give the free backwards pass on purpose, if that leaves the offence somewhere worse.</p>\n<p>Work <strong>with</strong> the mark, not on top of them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mark stopping the around break:</strong> the handler defender protects inside-out breaks across the front of the stack.</li>\n<li><strong>Mark set inside-out:</strong> the handler defender protects round the back.</li>\n<li><strong>Straight-up mark (stopping hucks):</strong> the handler defender takes all the upfield breaks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On the open side, either commit to the lane (forcing cuts wider and towards the sideline) or stay with your player. Don't drift into no man's land where you do neither.</p>\n\n<h2>Handler switches</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Give-and-go switch:</strong> the thrower passes to the open-side reset and cuts up the line. The mark follows the disc on a path that shuts off the break side; the reset's defender picks up the up-line cutter.</li>\n<li><strong>Plinko switch:</strong> the thrower swings to a reset behind and cuts round the back to the break side. The mark follows the disc and screens the break side straight away; the other defender picks up the free player, approaching as if setting a force in case the mark jumped off early.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tell your mark \"switches!\" when you're set up to help, so you're both ready.</p>\n\n<h2>Set-up against a horizontal stack</h2>\n<p>Stoppages, bricks and slow set-ups after a turnover let the defence get organised and absorb the first options.</p>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"hd-ho\"></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mark:</strong> inside-out force to pressure upfield throws.</li>\n<li><strong>Handler defenders:</strong> help in the lanes early, then close in late, or keep protecting upfield and give the lateral throw.</li>\n<li><strong>Open-side defender:</strong> mark underneath. You have deep help, so don't get beaten under.</li>\n<li><strong>Break-side defender:</strong> protect the middle of the field from in-cuts by either break-side player.</li>\n<li><strong>Deep guard:</strong> track the deep cuts while still nominally marking a break-side player; recover when the disc moves.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the sideline</strong> the pressure goes up: force the line, stay tight on the reset (don't get beaten up the line), stand between the disc and the third handler to stop the swing, and give up small gains that keep the disc on the line rather than allow a break. Against strong throwers, a straight-up force on the line stops the hucks instead: mark the reset on the inside so they have to cut up and away.</p>\n\n<h2>Set-up against a vertical stack</h2>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"hd-vert\"></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mark:</strong> inside-out force to stop the narrow breaks into the stack.</li>\n<li><strong>Reset and front-of-stack defenders:</strong> stay tight. Watch for the little flip to the break side.</li>\n<li><strong>Two \"unders\":</strong> share the open-side in-cuts. One takes the first cut straight to the open side; the other starts wider and takes the wider cuts.</li>\n<li><strong>Break side:</strong> take any in-cut on that side.</li>\n<li><strong>Deep guard:</strong> at the back with eyes on the disc, calling switches for anyone needing help deep.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Once you're actively marking a cut, that's your player: stay with them unless they abort back into the stack or you can hand them off with a switch. As players leave the stack, the remaining defenders tighten up until everyone has one each. Nobody should be left unmarked.</p>\n<p>If a throw does get out, defenders will be out of position: work hard to recover, get a force on and contain.</p>\n<p>Practise with <a href=\"#drill-marking-as-a-pair\">Marking as a Pair</a>, <a href=\"#drill-handler-help-lanes\">Handler Help in the Lanes</a>, <a href=\"#drill-handler-switches\">Handler Switches</a> and <a href=\"#drill-bracket-back-of-stack\">Bracketing the Back of the Stack</a>.</p>\n"}, {"slug": "wall-zone", "title": "Wall Zone (1-3-3) & Zone Principles", "hero": null, "html": "\n<p class=\"lede\">Think of a zone as a machine for making turnovers. It needs a clear purpose, and every player needs to know their part in it. This page covers the most common outdoor zone, the 1-3-3 wall (also called \"junk\"), and the principles behind any zone. For other shapes see <a href=\"#outdoor-zones\">Outdoor Zones</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Purpose</h2>\n<p>Push the offence towards a sideline, put heavy pressure on the thrower there and stop throws back to the middle. Turnovers come from handlers trying to break through the wall, or from hopeful high-count throws to our deep defenders.</p>\n<div class=\"tipbox\"><strong>Contain</strong>: every time the disc moves, delay the offence so the wall can get set. <strong>Pressure</strong>: once set, make them take sideways passes towards the sideline. <strong>Trap</strong>: once they're on the line, keep them there and keep the count going.</div>\n\n<h2>Roles</h2>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"wz-middle\"></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chase:</strong> follows the disc and forces towards the nearer sideline. Aggressive in the middle to stop easy upfield passes, and gets between the disc and any handler cutting into the space in front of the wall. On the sideline, moves round the back to stop the swing off the line.</li>\n<li><strong>Wall (three players):</strong> stand within about 1.5 m of each other and follow the disc, forming an instant barrier to upfield throws. Stay together: a hole in the wall is what zone offences look for. Even a slow wall that stays tight forces the disc round it, towards the sideline, rather than through the middle. The <strong>middle of the wall</strong> sets the wall's distance from the disc, keeps it together and can \"crash\" on a player cutting into the space in front of it (then recover). Standing side-on helps them see cuts behind.</li>\n<li><strong>Mids (two):</strong> each patrol one side behind the wall. You're only doing a job if you're marking someone in your area. With two attackers in your space, stand where it's hard to hit either. When the disc goes to your sideline, block the up-line throw past the wall (sneak a look behind you). The far mid drifts across to pressure players behind the wall, but watches for the big cross-field swing.</li>\n<li><strong>Deep:</strong> stays just deeper than the deepest attacker so long throws are always low-percentage. When the disc is trapped on the sideline, expect the desperate long shot: that's your block.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>The sideline trap</h2>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"wz-trap\"></div>\n\n<h2>Positioning and talking</h2>\n<p>Good zone players keep moving, opening a space for a moment and shutting it again before the offence can use it. A zone that looks a bit chaotic is harder to read than a rigid one. Talk in a <strong>cascade</strong>: the sideline talks to the deep and the chase; the deep talks to the mids; the mids talk to the middle of the wall; the middle of the wall talks to the wall.</p>\n<p><strong>How tight should the wall be?</strong> In wind, stand close to the disc: throws over the top are risky, so press the short gains and let the mids pinch into the middle. In calm conditions, sit further back to make throws into the middle harder and help the mids, who may need to mark the wide players more tightly.</p>\n\n<h2>When the wall is broken</h2>\n<p>Once a throw gets round, through or over the wall, the offence usually has more players upfield than you. The priority is to <strong>delay</strong>: stop the next upfield pass so the wall can catch up. Don't chase the disc; get to where the next pass is going. The mids sprint to the upfield space, the deep watches for a quick long shot, and the chase and wall get between the disc and the middle of the field, then set up again.</p>\n\n<h2>Switching from zone to person defence</h2>\n<p>Reasons to switch: the offence is near the end zone (person D pressures a small space better), you only wanted the zone to stop a huck off the pull, you want to unsettle them mid-point, or the zone isn't working. A good moment is just after a pass to the sideline, when they have the fewest options. The deep often makes the call, or you agree to switch after a set number of passes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Repeat the call loudly</strong> several times, sideline included, so everyone hears it.</li>\n<li><strong>Pick up the biggest threats first,</strong> not the nearest player: the deepest attacker (usually the deep's job), cutters close to the disc, then a mark on the disc.</li>\n<li><strong>Point at your player</strong> so nobody is double-covered, and shout about anyone left free.</li>\n<li>Aim: everyone has a player within five seconds and no free pass is given away.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Variations</h2>\n<p>Small changes to the same zone make you harder to read without learning a new one:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Middle of the wall drops behind:</strong> a three-person cup around the disc with extra cover behind. More throws around the disc are allowed, but deep receivers are well covered. Good in calm weather to stop throws over the top.</li>\n<li><strong>Middle of the wall marks the reset:</strong> no easy dump, so handlers have to take on the cup. Good in wind against a team happy to make small gains.</li>\n<li><strong>Three mids, no deep:</strong> fills the space behind the wall and dares them to huck. Good against weaker throwers or in difficult conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>Change by field position:</strong> e.g. when the wind blows hard towards one sideline, have the outside of the wall mark the reset whenever the disc goes to that line.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Strengths and weaknesses</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Good:</strong> four players around the disc make flat upfield throws hard; very effective on the sideline in wind.</li>\n<li><strong>Watch out for:</strong> experienced teams know it well; there are no handler marks, so patient handler movement can work it upfield; there's space behind the wall for overheads; and with so many players around the disc, a throw beyond the wall often finds the offence outnumbering you.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Practise with <a href=\"#drill-zone-moving-unit\">Moving as a Unit</a>, <a href=\"#drill-beat-the-wall\">Beat the Wall</a>, <a href=\"#drill-box-trolls\">Box Trolls</a>, <a href=\"#drill-zone-jailbreak-game\">the Jailbreak Game</a>, <a href=\"#drill-zone-to-match-game\">Zone-to-Match Transition</a> and <a href=\"#drill-junk-zone-roles\">Junk Zone: Roles and Talk</a>.</p>\n"}, {"slug": "hybrid-defences", "title": "Hybrid Defences: Fred & Wilma", "hero": null, "html": "\n<p class=\"lede\">Hybrid defences mix tight marking with zone-style positions. Some roles guard an area (like a zone deep), others guard a player (like \"don't let the reset get it\"). They work because they look a lot like person defence, so an offence that isn't ready for a defender lurking in the lane gets caught out. Talk constantly: if your player drifts out of your area, hand them on and recover.</p>\n\n<h2>Fred</h2>\n<p>A containing defence: take away upfield throws and continuations, so the offence has to make lots of passes or take on big throws.</p>\n<div class=\"pdia\" data-key=\"hy-fred\"></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Line-up:</strong> four handler defenders, two mids and a deep. One handler defender becomes \"Fred\" and stands right in the open-side throwing lane, calling \"PUSH\" or \"DROP\" to the other handler defenders to close the upfield space.</li>\n<li><strong>Allow sideways and backwards passes,</strong> but work hard to stop the next throw going upfield.</li>\n<li><strong>Force back to Fred.</strong> The mark always forces so that Fred is on the open side: straight up or middle on the sideline, changing direction in the middle of the field.</li>\n<li><strong>Fred is whoever gets there.</strong> When the disc moves, all the handler defenders sprint to flood the upfield space first, then sort out roles. The two nearest the disc take the mark and Fred; shout \"I'M FRED!\". If the best-placed Fred would leave a hole in front of the disc while the mark arrives, they step across into the mark instead and the sprinting defenders fill the gap.</li>\n<li><strong>Against aggressive handlers</strong> (give-and-goes), handler defenders get tight on anyone cutting upfield, then drop off again when they settle behind or level with the disc.</li>\n<li><strong>Against big throws over the top,</strong> the mids and deep (your best in the air) position so a throw has to beat them too, and Fred is well placed to pressure deep throws.</li>\n<li><strong>On the sideline:</strong> you'd rather give the big cross-field swing than anything to the middle. The far handler defender and the far wing drift infield; one handler defender gets tight on the reset. Wing and deep pounce on any swing that drifts or hangs. If the swing is completed, the wing gets in the way of the continuation until the handler defenders recover.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Fred works best in the first third of the pitch: few teams are patient enough to make small gains all the way up, and a good pull plus a hard Fred can earn Callahans near their own end zone. If they get past halfway, consider switching defence.</p>\n\n<h2>Wilma</h2>\n<p>Looks almost the same as Fred, which is the point: switching between them confuses offences that think they've worked you out. But Wilma is aggressive, aiming to make blocks rather than wait for errors. It is close to the FSU zone on <a href=\"#outdoor-zones\">Outdoor Zones</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Handler defenders stay tight</strong> to stop the easy reset and make the handlers cut upfield into the space in front of the disc. They always cover the disc and the two biggest threats around it, switching as those change.</li>\n<li><strong>The mark always forces towards Wilma,</strong> sending the disc back at our defenders.</li>\n<li><strong>Wilma</strong> stands in the lane close to the disc (but not so close it's a double team), mobile and ready to lay out. Get side-on to see cutters behind you, and anticipate handler cuts in front of you so you can crash.</li>\n<li><strong>Mids</strong> mark cutters in their area and protect underneath, as there's little help from the handler defenders.</li>\n<li><strong>Deep</strong> plays deep guard, switching onto the deepest threat and handing in-cuts to the mids or Wilma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Practise with <a href=\"#drill-fred-walkthrough\">the Fred Walk-Through</a> and <a href=\"#drill-wilma-box\">the Wilma Box</a>.</p>\n"}];
